insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('understanding-your-emotions', 'understanding-emotions', 'wellness', 'article', 'Understanding Your Emotions: What They Are and What They Are Telling You', 'Emotions are a normal part of being human. Learning to notice and name them can make hard days easier to handle.', 'Emotions can feel confusing, loud, or hard to explain. You might feel angry and not know why. You might feel fine on the outside and tight in your chest on the inside. This article is a plain-language look at what emotions are and how you can get to know yours a little better.

## What an emotion is

The American Psychological Association (APA) describes an emotion as a complex reaction pattern with three parts: what you feel, what your body does, and what you are moved to do. A racing heart, a knot in your stomach, and the urge to leave a room can all be pieces of one emotion.

The APA also notes that the emotion you feel depends on what a situation means to you. If something looks like a threat, fear is more likely. If you think someone disapproves of you, shame is more likely. That is one reason two people can react very differently to the same event.

## Emotions are not good or bad

Sadness, anger, fear, and joy are all part of a normal emotional life. Each one gives you information. Fear can point to something that feels unsafe. Anger can point to something that feels unfair. Sadness can point to something you care about and have lost. You do not have to like every feeling. You also do not have to act on every feeling.

The World Health Organization (WHO) describes mental health as a state of well-being that lets people cope with the stresses of life. That does not mean feeling happy all the time. It means being able to cope, and part of coping is being able to feel what you feel.

## Your situation matters

Feelings do not happen in a vacuum. Money worries, unsafe housing, discrimination, a hard job, or a health problem can all shape how you feel. The WHO notes that poverty, violence, and inequality raise the risk of mental health problems, while safe neighborhoods and strong community ties are protective. If you feel low or on edge because life is genuinely hard, that is not a personal failing. It is a reasonable response to real conditions.

## Getting to know your feelings

A few simple habits can help:

- **Pause and check in.** Ask yourself, "What am I feeling right now?" Try to be more specific than "bad" or "fine."
- **Notice your body.** Where do you feel it? Tight shoulders, a heavy chest, or a warm face can be clues.
- **Name it.** Words like "disappointed," "overwhelmed," "lonely," or "irritated" can make a feeling feel less like a fog.
- **Ask what it needs.** Rest? A conversation? A boundary? Sometimes it just needs to be noticed.
- **Look for patterns.** You might see that certain times of day, places, or people tend to bring up certain feelings.

The National Institute of Mental Health (NIMH) says self-care looks different for each person and that it can take trial and error to find what works for you. The same is true for working with emotions. There is no single right way.

## When feelings feel like too much

Strong feelings that pass are normal. Feelings that stay intense, make it hard to sleep, eat, work, or be around people, or last for two weeks or more are worth bringing to a professional. NIMH lists this as a sign to seek help. You do not need to wait until things are unbearable.

**Example (fictional):** Dana snaps at a coworker after a meeting and then feels guilty all evening. When Dana pauses to check in, the feeling underneath is not just anger. It is worry about losing the job after hearing rumors of layoffs. Naming the worry does not fix the layoffs. It does help Dana decide what to do next, like talking to a manager, and it makes an apology to the coworker easier to give.

Learning about your emotions is a skill, and like any skill it grows with practice. Be patient with yourself. Some days will be easier than others.', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["Emotions involve thoughts, body sensations, and urges to act, not just a mood.","No emotion is wrong. Feelings give information, and you can notice a feeling without acting on it.","Real-life circumstances like money, safety, and work stress shape how you feel.","Naming a feeling in specific words can make it easier to handle.","Feelings that are intense or last two weeks or more are worth sharing with a professional."]'::jsonb, '["What emotion have I felt most often this week, and what might it be telling me?","Where do I notice emotions in my body?","Which feelings was I taught to hide, and is that still helping me?"]'::jsonb, '**A one-minute check-in**

You can do this sitting down, standing in line, or before bed.

1. Pause and take one slow breath.
2. Ask: *What am I feeling right now?* Try to find a specific word. If you get stuck, pick from a few: tense, sad, restless, annoyed, hopeful, tired, worried, calm.
3. Ask: *Where do I feel it in my body?*
4. Ask: *What might this feeling need?* (For example: a break, a glass of water, a talk with someone, or nothing at all.)
5. Take another slow breath and let the answer be good enough.

If this brings up feelings that are too strong, stop and do something that grounds you, like feeling your feet on the floor. This exercise is for everyday awareness. It is not a substitute for professional care.', '[{"title":"Emotions","publisher":"American Psychological Association","year":"n.d.","url":"https://www.apa.org/topics/emotions"},{"title":"Mental health (fact sheet)","publisher":"World Health Organization","year":"2026","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response"},{"title":"Caring for Your Mental Health","publisher":"National Institute of Mental Health","year":"n.d.","url":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/topics/caring-for-your-mental-health"}]'::jsonb, '["emotional-regulation-skills","self-compassion-basics","seeking-professional-help"]'::jsonb, array['mental wellness','article']::text[], false, '', 'If strong emotions are hard to manage or last two weeks or more, talk with your primary care provider or a licensed mental health professional. If you are in crisis or thinking about suicide, call or text 988 (the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) or chat at chat.988lifeline.org, any time, in the U.S. For a life-threatening emergency, call 911.', 'This is general education. Emotion research is still developing, and people experience and describe emotions differently across cultures and situations.', 3, true, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('understanding-stress', 'stress', 'wellness', 'article', 'Understanding Stress: What It Does and What Can Help', 'Stress is the body''s response to pressure. This article explains how it works, when it becomes a problem, and small steps that may help.', 'Almost everyone feels stress sometimes. A deadline, a bill, a sick family member, or a big change can all bring it on. Stress is not a weakness. It is a response to demands. This article explains what stress is, why it can wear you down, and what may help.

## What stress is

The National Institute of Mental Health (NIMH) describes stress as the physical or mental response to an external cause, such as a heavy workload or an illness. It can be short-lived or it can last a long time. The American Psychological Association (APA) describes it as a response to internal or external stressors and notes that it is a typical reaction to everyday pressure that becomes a problem when it disrupts day-to-day functioning.

Stress can show up in the body. The APA lists signs such as a racing heartbeat, sweating, dry mouth, and shortness of breath. It can also show up in how you think and act, like feeling short-tempered, having trouble focusing, or avoiding things.

## Stress and anxiety are related

NIMH explains that stress usually fades once the situation is resolved. Anxiety is the body''s reaction to stress and can stick around even when there is no current threat. When anxiety does not go away and gets in the way of daily life, it can affect sleep and other body systems, and it can raise the risk of an anxiety disorder or depression.

## Stress that comes from real life

Not all stress can be solved with a breathing exercise. Some stress comes from conditions that are outside your control: low pay, unstable housing, caring for others, discrimination, or health problems. The APA''s Stress in America reports have pointed to societal division as a source of stress for many U.S. adults. If your stress has real causes, it makes sense that it feels heavy. Coping skills can help you get through it, but they do not replace changes to the situation, support from others, or practical help.

## Things that may help

NIMH and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) suggest a number of general steps:

- **Learn your triggers.** Notice what tends to raise your stress and what helps. NIMH says this may take trial and error.
- **Move your body.** The CDC suggests building up to about 2.5 hours of physical activity per week, and says it is fine to start small.
- **Protect your sleep.** Keep a regular schedule. The CDC says adults need 7 or more hours per night.
- **Take breaks from the news.** The CDC suggests limiting news and social media if constant updates upset you.
- **Relax on purpose.** Breathing, stretching, journaling, time outdoors, and hobbies are all options.
- **Stay connected.** Lean on friends, family, or community and faith groups you trust.
- **Go easy on alcohol and caffeine.** Both can affect sleep and mood.

Pick one or two things that feel doable. You do not need to change everything at once.

## Signs it may be time to get support

NIMH says stress may be a sign to seek help when it disrupts everyday life, leads you to avoid activities, or seems to be always present. The CDC also says that if you are struggling to cope, it is a good idea to look for more support.

**Example (fictional):** Marcus works two jobs and cares for his mother. He notices he is not sleeping and has stopped seeing friends. He cannot quit a job, so he starts small. He takes a ten-minute walk after his shift, texts one friend a week, and asks his sister to cover one evening. His situation is still hard, but he feels a little less alone in it. Later, when the sleep problems continue, he talks with his doctor.

Stress is a normal part of life, but you do not have to carry it alone. Small steps and support from others can make a real difference.', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["Stress is a normal response to pressure, but long-lasting stress can affect sleep, mood, and health.","Stress often fades when a situation resolves. Anxiety can linger even without a current threat.","Real-life causes of stress deserve real-life support, not just self-help tips.","Small steps like regular sleep, movement, breaks from news, and staying connected may help.","If stress disrupts daily life or never lets up, it is a good time to talk with a professional."]'::jsonb, '["What are the top two or three things stressing me right now, and which can I change, share, or set down for a while?","What has helped me through stress in the past?","Who could I ask for practical help this week?"]'::jsonb, '**A stress sort**

Take five minutes with a piece of paper.

1. Write down everything that is stressing you, big or small.
2. Draw two columns: **I can change this** and **I can''t change this right now**.
3. Sort each item into a column.
4. Under "I can change this," pick one tiny next step, like sending an email, making a call, or asking for help.
5. Under "I can''t change this right now," write one thing that makes it easier to carry, such as a walk, talking to a friend, or setting a limit on news.

Stop if the list feels overwhelming. You can do half today and half tomorrow. This is a planning tool and does not replace professional support.', '[{"title":"I''m So Stressed Out! Fact Sheet","publisher":"National Institute of Mental Health","year":"n.d.","url":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/so-stressed-out-fact-sheet"},{"title":"Managing Stress","publisher":"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention","year":"n.d.","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/mental-health/living-with/index.html"},{"title":"Stress","publisher":"American Psychological Association","year":"n.d.","url":"https://www.apa.org/topics/stress"}]'::jsonb, '["understanding-anxiety","burnout-signs-and-recovery","sleep-routines-that-help"]'::jsonb, array['mental wellness','article']::text[], false, '', 'If stress keeps you from daily activities, is always present, or comes with trouble sleeping, hopelessness, or physical symptoms, talk with your doctor or a licensed mental health professional. If you are in crisis or thinking about suicide, call or text 988 (the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) or chat at chat.988lifeline.org, any time, in the U.S. For a life-threatening emergency, call 911.', 'This is general education based on public health and psychology organizations. Stress affects people differently, and what helps varies from person to person.', 3, true, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('understanding-anxiety', 'anxiety', 'wellness', 'article', 'Understanding Anxiety: When Worry Gets Heavy', 'Anxiety is a normal feeling, but sometimes it gets too big or lasts too long. Learn what anxiety can look like and where to find help.', 'Feeling anxious now and then is part of being human. You might feel it before a test, a medical appointment, or a big conversation. But for some people, anxiety is stronger, shows up more often, or does not go away. This article explains the difference and what kinds of help exist. It is general education only and cannot tell you whether you have an anxiety disorder.

## Everyday anxiety and anxiety disorders

The National Institute of Mental Health (NIMH) says that feeling anxious is a normal part of life, but anxiety disorders involve more than occasional worry or fear. In an anxiety disorder, the anxiety does not go away, may be felt in many situations, and can get worse over time. It can get in the way of work, school, and relationships. NIMH names several types, including generalized anxiety disorder, panic disorder, social anxiety disorder, and phobia-related disorders.

Anxiety disorders are common. NIMH reports that a third of U.S. adolescents and adults experience an anxiety disorder at some point in their lives. The World Health Organization (WHO) estimates that anxiety disorders affected 470 million people worldwide in 2023, and calls them the most common mental disorders.

## What anxiety can feel like

According to the WHO, anxiety can include excessive fear or worry, trouble concentrating or deciding, irritability, restlessness, nausea or stomach discomfort, a pounding heart, sweating, trembling, sleep problems, and a sense of danger. People often start avoiding the places or situations that bring it on. Avoiding can bring short-term relief, but it can make life smaller over time.

## A note on panic

Some people have sudden waves of intense fear, often called panic attacks. These can feel scary and physical. The WHO describes panic disorder as involving panic attacks and a fear of having more of them. If you have these episodes, it is a good idea to talk with a health care provider. Chest pain, trouble breathing, and other physical symptoms deserve a medical check, and a provider can help sort out the cause. If you think you are having a medical emergency, call 911.

## Real-world reasons for anxiety

Anxiety is not always about "overthinking." Sometimes the world really is uncertain. Unstable work, money trouble, unsafe neighborhoods, discrimination, caregiving, and health worries are all reasons a person might feel on edge. The WHO notes that social and environmental conditions affect mental health. Feeling anxious in hard circumstances is understandable.

## What can help

The WHO says highly effective treatments exist for anxiety disorders. It lists psychological therapy, especially cognitive-behavioural therapy (CBT), as having the most evidence. Therapy can be delivered in person, online, or through self-help materials. Some people also use medication, which is a decision to make with a licensed health care provider. The WHO notes that many people benefit from skills like relaxation and mindfulness too, as part of a bigger plan.

The WHO also lists self-care steps that may support anxiety management:

- Keep regular eating and sleeping routines.
- Move your body, even for a few minutes.
- Practice slow breathing or muscle relaxation.
- Limit or avoid alcohol and illicit drugs, which can make anxiety worse.
- Try a few minutes of mindfulness each day.

These steps can be useful, but they are not a substitute for professional treatment when anxiety is interfering with your life.

## The treatment gap

The WHO reports that only about 1 in 4 people who need treatment for anxiety (27.6%) get any. If you have thought about getting help and have not, you are far from alone. Cost, access, stigma, and not knowing where to start are all real barriers.

**Example (fictional):** Priya notices that her worry about work has spread. She now avoids driving on the highway and wakes up at 3 a.m. with a pounding heart. She tells her primary care doctor, who asks questions and suggests a referral for therapy. Priya also tries a few minutes of slow breathing before bed. The therapy is the main support. The breathing is a small extra.

If anxiety is taking up a lot of space in your life, help is available and many people feel better with support.', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["Some anxiety is normal. It may be a concern when it is strong, lasts a long time, or gets in the way of daily life.","Anxiety disorders are common. NIMH reports about a third of U.S. adolescents and adults experience one at some point.","Panic attacks can feel frightening and physical. A health care provider can help you understand them.","Effective treatments exist, including therapy such as CBT. Self-care steps can support, but not replace, treatment.","Real-world stress and uncertainty can drive anxiety. It is not a personal failure."]'::jsonb, '["When do I notice anxiety most, and what is happening around me at those times?","Is anxiety making me avoid things that matter to me?","Who could I talk to about what I am feeling?"]'::jsonb, '**Slow breathing for a few minutes**

This is a general relaxation skill. It may take the edge off, but it does not treat an anxiety disorder.

1. Sit or lie in a comfortable position. If you want, place a hand on your belly.
2. Breathe in slowly through your nose for about 4 seconds.
3. Breathe out gently through your mouth for about 6 seconds. A longer exhale often feels calming.
4. Repeat for 2 to 5 minutes. If your mind wanders, that is normal. Just return to the breath.

Stop if you feel dizzy or more anxious, and return to normal breathing. If you ever have chest pain, trouble breathing, or think you may be having a medical emergency, call 911.', '[{"title":"Anxiety Disorders","publisher":"National Institute of Mental Health","year":"2024","url":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/topics/anxiety-disorders"},{"title":"Anxiety disorders (fact sheet)","publisher":"World Health Organization","year":"2026","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/anxiety-disorders"},{"title":"I''m So Stressed Out! Fact Sheet","publisher":"National Institute of Mental Health","year":"n.d.","url":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/so-stressed-out-fact-sheet"}]'::jsonb, '["understanding-stress","mindfulness-basics","seeking-professional-help"]'::jsonb, array['mental wellness','article']::text[], true, 'This article discusses panic and anxiety symptoms. Take a break if it feels like too much.', 'If anxiety is hard to control, keeps you from daily activities, or comes with panic attacks, talk with your doctor or a licensed mental health professional. To find providers, call SAMHSA''s National Helpline at 1-800-662-HELP (4357) or visit findtreatment.gov. If you are in crisis or thinking about suicide, call or text 988 (the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) or chat at chat.988lifeline.org, any time, in the U.S. For a life-threatening emergency, call 911.', 'This is general education and not a diagnosis. Treatment research is strong for some therapies, but results differ by person and by type of anxiety.', 4, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('understanding-depression', 'depression', 'wellness', 'article', 'Understanding Depression: More Than a Bad Mood', 'Depression is common and treatable. This article explains what it can look like, what causes it, and how to get support.', 'Everyone feels sad sometimes. Depression is different. It is more than a rough week or a passing low mood, and it is not a sign of weakness or something you can simply "snap out of." This article gives general information about depression. It cannot diagnose you or anyone else.

## What depression is

The National Institute of Mental Health (NIMH) describes depression, also called major depressive disorder or clinical depression, as more than normal sadness. It can cause severe symptoms that affect how you feel, think, and handle daily activities like sleeping, eating, and working. NIMH says it can affect anyone, regardless of age, race, income, culture, or education.

The World Health Organization (WHO) says a depressive episode involves low mood (sadness, irritability, or a sense of emptiness) or a loss of interest or pleasure in activities. These feelings last most of the day, nearly every day, for at least two weeks. Other possible signs include trouble concentrating, strong guilt or feelings of low self-worth, hopelessness, sleep changes, appetite or weight changes, and low energy. Some people have thoughts of death or suicide.

## How common it is

The WHO estimates that about 5.2% of adults worldwide live with depression. That is around 322 million people. The WHO also reports that in high-income countries, only about one third of people with depression receive mental health treatment. If you have been struggling alone, you are not the only one.

## What contributes to it

NIMH says research suggests that genetic, biological, environmental, and psychological factors all play a role. Depression is rarely caused by just one thing. Loss, long-term stress, isolation, financial strain, health problems, and unsafe or unfair living conditions can all be part of the picture. Depression is not anyone''s fault, and it is not caused by a lack of willpower or gratitude.

## Treatment works

Depression is treatable. The WHO says effective treatments exist for mild, moderate, and severe depression, and that psychological treatments (talk therapy) are a first-line option. NIMH lists medications, talk therapy including telehealth and online options, and brain stimulation therapy as treatment options. Which approach fits depends on the person, and that is a decision to make with a licensed health care provider. If you take medication or are in treatment, keep going and talk with your provider before changing anything.

## Things that can support you

Alongside professional care, the WHO suggests staying active, keeping up activities you enjoy and social contact, keeping regular sleep and eating routines, avoiding alcohol and illicit drugs, and talking with someone you trust. When depression is heavy, even these can feel too hard. If so, start tiny. A short walk, a text to a friend, or a glass of water counts. These steps support care. They are not a replacement for it.

## If you are thinking about suicide

Some people with depression have thoughts of suicide. If this is you, you are not alone and you deserve support right now. NIMH says to call or text 988, or chat at 988lifeline.org. In a life-threatening situation, call 911. Reaching out to someone you trust is also a good step.

**Example (fictional):** Jordan has felt flat for about a month. Nothing feels fun, sleep is a mess, and getting dressed takes all of Jordan''s energy. A coworker mentions she saw her doctor about something similar. Jordan makes an appointment, writes down the symptoms beforehand, and brings a friend along for support. The doctor takes Jordan seriously and goes over treatment options. It is a first step, not an overnight fix.

Depression can make it feel like nothing will change. That feeling is a symptom, not a fact. Many people recover with support, and reaching out is a strong thing to do.', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["Depression is a real, common health condition, not a character flaw.","Symptoms such as low mood or loss of interest that last most of the day for two weeks or more are worth bringing to a provider.","Multiple factors contribute, including biology, life events, and living conditions.","Effective treatments exist, and you should talk with a provider before making any change to treatment.","If you are thinking about suicide, call or text 988 or chat at 988lifeline.org. In an emergency, call 911."]'::jsonb, '["Have I noticed changes in my sleep, energy, interest, or mood that have lasted a while?","Who in my life could I tell how I have really been feeling?","What is one small, kind thing I can do for myself today?"]'::jsonb, '**One small step**

When everything feels heavy, this exercise lowers the bar on purpose.

1. Write down three tiny actions that would take under five minutes, such as drinking water, opening a window, stepping outside, or texting someone "thinking of you."
2. Pick just one and do it now, or when you are ready today.
3. Notice how it felt, without judging. Even "nothing changed" is fine information.
4. Come back tomorrow and choose again.

This is a gentle way to build a little momentum. It is not a treatment for depression. If you are struggling for two weeks or more, or you are having thoughts of suicide, please reach out to a professional or call or text 988.', '[{"title":"Depression","publisher":"National Institute of Mental Health","year":"n.d.","url":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/topics/depression"},{"title":"Depressive disorder (depression)","publisher":"World Health Organization","year":"2026","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/depression"},{"title":"Help for Mental Illnesses","publisher":"National Institute of Mental Health","year":"n.d.","url":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/find-help"}]'::jsonb, '["understanding-loneliness","seeking-professional-help","grief-and-loss"]'::jsonb, array['mental wellness','article']::text[], true, 'This article discusses depression and mentions suicide. If you are in distress, you can call or text 988 at any time in the U.S.', 'If low mood, loss of interest, or hopelessness lasts two weeks or more, talk with your doctor or a licensed mental health professional. Do not stop or change any treatment without talking to your provider. If you are in crisis or thinking about suicide, call or text 988 (the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) or chat at chat.988lifeline.org, any time, in the U.S. For a life-threatening emergency, call 911.', 'This is general education and not a diagnosis. Treatments help many people, but responses vary, and finding the right fit can take time.', 4, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('emotional-regulation-skills', 'emotional-regulation', 'wellness', 'article', 'Emotional Regulation: Working With Big Feelings', 'Emotional regulation is about how we respond to feelings, not getting rid of them. Learn what the research says and some gentle skills to try.', 'When people hear "emotional regulation," they sometimes think it means staying calm all the time or pushing feelings down. It does not. Emotional regulation is about how you respond to the feelings you have, and it is a skill most people keep building throughout life.

## What it means

Emotion regulation is the set of things people do to influence which emotions they have, when they have them, and how they experience and express them. Some strategies happen before a feeling gets big, like planning ahead or leaving a stressful situation. Others happen once a feeling is already there, like taking a breath or reframing a thought.

## Not all strategies work the same

A well-known 2010 meta-analysis in *Clinical Psychology Review* by Aldao, Nolen-Hoeksema, and Schweizer combined 241 effect sizes from 114 studies. It looked at how six strategies related to symptoms of anxiety, depression, eating problems, and substance-related problems. The authors found that rumination (going over and over a problem) had a large link with symptoms. Avoidance, problem solving, and suppression had medium-to-large links. Reappraisal and acceptance had smaller links.

It is important not to over-read this. These were links, not proof of cause. A person who feels very distressed may ruminate more, and rumination may add to distress, or both. The study also looked at patterns across groups, not what will work for you in a particular moment. Still, it suggests that stewing on a problem or pushing a feeling away may not be the most helpful go-to moves for many people.

## Skills you can practice

The National Institute of Mental Health (NIMH) and the American Psychological Association (APA) point to a few general tools:

- **Slow your body down.** NIMH lists deep breathing and relaxation exercises among ways to manage stress.
- **Name what you feel.** Putting a word to an emotion can make it feel less overwhelming.
- **Take a break.** If you are flooded, stepping away for a few minutes can help you come back more clearly.
- **Challenge unhelpful thoughts.** NIMH suggests noticing and questioning negative or unhelpful thinking.
- **Practice mindfulness.** The APA describes mindfulness as paying attention to the present moment and observing thoughts and feelings without judgment.
- **Move and rest.** Regular activity and consistent sleep make feelings easier to manage.
- **Reach out.** Talking with someone you trust can help.

## Regulation is not suppression

Being able to regulate emotions does not mean you should hide how you feel or put up with harm. Sometimes a strong feeling is an accurate signal. Anger at being treated unfairly or fear in an unsafe situation may be telling you something real. The goal is to feel what you feel and then choose what to do with it, not to feel nothing.

It also helps to be honest about what is outside your control. Your ability to stay steady is shaped by sleep, safety, money, health, and support. If you are exhausted, in pain, or in a hard situation, big feelings are more likely. That is not a failure of willpower.

**Example (fictional):** Sam gets a harsh email and feels heat rise in his face. His usual move is to fire back right away. This time he notices the heat, takes three slow breaths, and steps outside for five minutes. When he returns, he is still upset, but he writes a calmer reply that sticks to the facts. He did not get rid of the anger. He just gave himself a bit more choice about what to do next.

## When to get more support

If strong emotions often feel out of control, lead to harm to you or others, or are tied to past trauma, a licensed mental health professional can teach skills in a safe, structured way. Therapy is not a sign that you have failed at coping. It is a way to learn more tools.

Building this skill takes time, and some days will go better than others. That is normal.', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["Emotional regulation means influencing how you respond to feelings, not erasing them.","A large research review linked rumination, avoidance, and suppression with more symptoms. These are associations, not proof of cause.","Pausing, breathing, naming feelings, and reaching out are simple starting points.","Sleep, safety, and support affect how steady you can be. Hard circumstances make big feelings more likely.","If feelings often feel out of control, a licensed professional can teach skills safely."]'::jsonb, '["What do I usually do when a strong feeling hits, and does it help?","When is it easier or harder for me to stay steady?","Who can I turn to when I need to settle down?"]'::jsonb, '**Pause, name, choose**

Use this when a feeling is rising.

1. **Pause.** Stop what you are doing. Feel your feet on the floor.
2. **Breathe.** Take three slow breaths, with the exhale a little longer than the inhale.
3. **Name.** Silently say, "I''m feeling ___." Use a specific word if you can.
4. **Choose.** Ask: *What is one thing I can do that I will feel okay about later?* It might be waiting, walking away, asking for a minute, or saying what you need calmly.

You will not do this perfectly, and you do not have to. Practice when feelings are small so the steps are familiar when they are big. If you are in danger or feel like you might hurt yourself or someone else, seek help right away. This exercise is not a replacement for professional care.', '[{"title":"Emotion-regulation strategies across psychopathology: A meta-analytic review","publisher":"Clinical Psychology Review","year":"2010","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20015584/"},{"title":"I''m So Stressed Out! Fact Sheet","publisher":"National Institute of Mental Health","year":"n.d.","url":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/so-stressed-out-fact-sheet"},{"title":"Mindfulness meditation: A research-proven way to reduce stress","publisher":"American Psychological Association","year":"2019","url":"https://www.apa.org/topics/mindfulness/meditation"}]'::jsonb, '["understanding-your-emotions","mindfulness-basics","self-compassion-basics"]'::jsonb, array['mental wellness','article']::text[], false, '', 'If strong emotions often feel out of control, affect your safety or relationships, or follow a traumatic event, consider talking with a licensed mental health professional. If you are in crisis or thinking about suicide, call or text 988 (the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) or chat at chat.988lifeline.org, any time, in the U.S. For a life-threatening emergency, call 911.', 'The main research cited here is a meta-analysis of associations, so it cannot show that any one strategy causes better or worse mental health. Results also vary by person and situation.', 4, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('self-compassion-basics', 'self-compassion', 'wellness', 'article', 'Self-Compassion: Treating Yourself Like Someone You Care About', 'Self-compassion means meeting your own struggles with kindness instead of harsh criticism. Here is what it is, what research suggests, and how to practice.', 'Many of us would never talk to a friend the way we talk to ourselves. After a mistake, a hard day, or a setback, the inner voice can get harsh: "What is wrong with you?" Self-compassion is the practice of responding to your own struggles with the kind of care you would offer someone you love.

## What self-compassion is

Researchers often describe self-compassion as having a few connected parts. The first is kindness toward yourself when things go wrong, instead of harsh self-judgment. The second is remembering that struggle and imperfection are part of being human, not proof that you are alone or broken. The third is noticing painful feelings with some balance, without ignoring them or being swept away by them.

Self-compassion is not self-pity, and it is not making excuses. You can care about doing better and still be gentle with yourself while you learn. For many people, kindness is actually what makes it safer to look honestly at a mistake.

## What the research suggests

A 2012 meta-analysis in *Clinical Psychology Review* by MacBeth and Gumley, titled "Exploring compassion: a meta-analysis of the association between self-compassion and psychopathology," pulled together studies on how self-compassion relates to mental health symptoms such as depression and anxiety. In plain terms, this kind of research looks at whether people who are more self-compassionate tend to report fewer symptoms.

It is important to be careful here. Studies like these show associations. They cannot tell us that self-compassion causes better mental health, and they do not show that it will work the same way for everyone. People who are struggling less may also find it easier to be kind to themselves. Self-compassion is best seen as one possible tool among many, not a cure.

## Why it can be hard

Some people worry that being kind to themselves will make them lazy or weak. Others grew up in homes, schools, or workplaces where harshness was the norm. Some face real discrimination or pressure from the outside, and the inner critic can echo those messages. If self-kindness feels strange or even uncomfortable at first, that is common. It is a skill that can take time, and it is okay to start small.

Self-compassion also does not mean accepting unfair treatment from others or staying in unsafe situations. It means you treat yourself with care while you figure out what you need.

## Ways to practice

The National Institute of Mental Health (NIMH) notes that self-care looks different for everyone and may take trial and error. A few ideas to try:

- **Notice the tone.** When you make a mistake, pay attention to how you talk to yourself. Is it a tone you would use with a friend?
- **Say something kind.** Try a short phrase such as "This is hard right now" or "Anyone in this situation would struggle."
- **Remember you are not alone.** Many people feel the same way you do. Hardship is part of being human.
- **Take a caring action.** Rest, eat, step outside, or ask for help, the way you would for someone else.
- **Write a letter.** Describe your situation as if writing to a friend who is going through the same thing.

**Example (fictional):** Lena forgets an important deadline at work. Her first thought is, "I''m so useless." She stops and asks what she would say to a coworker in the same spot. She would say, "That''s stressful. Let''s figure out how to fix it." She says that to herself, emails her manager to explain, and plans a reminder system. She still feels disappointed, but she is not piling on shame.

## When self-criticism is very loud

If your inner critic is constant, hard to quiet, or tied to depression, trauma, or thoughts of self-harm, kind phrases alone may not be enough. A licensed mental health professional can help, and there are therapy approaches built around compassion. Asking for help is itself an act of self-compassion.

Be patient with yourself as you try this. There is no wrong way, and small steps count.', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["Self-compassion means responding to your struggles with kindness, shared humanity, and balance.","It is not self-pity or lowering your standards. Kindness can make it easier to learn from mistakes.","Research on self-compassion shows links with mental health, but links are not proof of cause.","Small practices like a kind phrase or a caring action are good places to start.","If self-criticism is constant or tied to depression or self-harm, a professional can help."]'::jsonb, '["How do I talk to myself after a mistake, and where did that voice come from?","What would I say to a friend in my exact situation?","What is one caring thing I could do for myself today?"]'::jsonb, '**A kind-words break**

This takes about two minutes. Use it when you are being hard on yourself.

1. Notice the feeling. Say to yourself, "This is a hard moment."
2. Remind yourself you are not alone: "Other people feel this way too. It is part of being human."
3. Offer a kind phrase. Try: "May I be gentle with myself," or "I''m doing the best I can with what I have right now."
4. If you like, put a hand on your chest or arm for a moment and take a slow breath.

If these words feel fake or bring up strong emotions, that is okay. You can stop, or try a more neutral phrase like "I''m learning." This exercise is a gentle practice and not a substitute for professional care.', '[{"title":"Exploring compassion: a meta-analysis of the association between self-compassion and psychopathology","publisher":"Clinical Psychology Review","year":"2012","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cpr.2012.06.003"},{"title":"Caring for Your Mental Health","publisher":"National Institute of Mental Health","year":"n.d.","url":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/topics/caring-for-your-mental-health"}]'::jsonb, '["understanding-your-emotions","emotional-regulation-skills","understanding-depression"]'::jsonb, array['mental wellness','article']::text[], false, '', 'If harsh self-criticism feels constant, or comes with depression, past trauma, or thoughts of hurting yourself, please talk with a licensed mental health professional or your doctor. If you are in crisis or thinking about suicide, call or text 988 (the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) or chat at chat.988lifeline.org, any time, in the U.S. For a life-threatening emergency, call 911.', 'The research cited here is a meta-analysis of associations between self-compassion and symptoms, so it cannot show that self-compassion causes improvement. The three-part description of self-compassion above is a common way researchers describe it and is not taken from the cited paper. We read only the title and citation details of the paper, not its full results, so no numbers from it are reported here.', 4, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('mindfulness-basics', 'mindfulness', 'wellness', 'article', 'Mindfulness: What It Is and What the Research Says', 'Mindfulness means paying attention to the present moment without judging it. Here is a clear look at what it can and cannot do.', 'Mindfulness has become a popular word, and it is easy to find big claims about it. This article gives a calm, plain look at what mindfulness is, what research says so far, and how to try it safely.

## What mindfulness is

The National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NCCIH), part of the National Institutes of Health, describes mindfulness as a form of meditation centered on paying attention to the present moment without judging it. The American Psychological Association (APA) describes two parts: paying attention to the present, and observing thoughts and feelings without judgment.

You do not have to sit cross-legged or clear your mind. A common version is simply noticing your breath, the sounds in the room, or the feeling of your feet on the floor, and gently coming back when your mind wanders. Wandering is expected. Coming back is the practice.

## Structured programs

Some programs teach mindfulness in a set way. Mindfulness-based stress reduction (MBSR) is an eight-week program with group classes and home practice, according to the APA. Mindfulness-based cognitive therapy (MBCT) combines MBSR with parts of cognitive behavioral therapy. These programs are usually taught by trained instructors.

## What research suggests

NCCIH summarizes research this way. A 2018 analysis of over 12,000 participants found that mindfulness approaches did better than no treatment for anxiety and depression, and performed about as well as evidence-based therapies. NCCIH also reports that mindfulness improved sleep quality more than education-based treatments, though it was no different from cognitive behavioral therapy or exercise. For pain, results were mixed.

The APA''s article reports a review of more than 200 studies that found mindfulness-based therapy especially helpful for stress, anxiety, and depression, and notes that MBCT can significantly reduce relapse in people with a history of major depression.

## Limits and cautions

NCCIH is clear that much of the research is preliminary or not scientifically rigorous. Many different practices have been studied, which makes results hard to compare. Several analyses noted a high or unclear risk of bias, and few studies followed people for more than two months.

Mindfulness is generally considered low risk, but it is not risk free. NCCIH cites a 2020 review in which about 8% of participants reported a negative effect, a rate similar to psychological therapies. The most common negative effects were anxiety and depression. If sitting with your thoughts makes you feel worse, you do not have to push through. Try shorter sessions, eyes open, or a more active form like mindful walking, or talk with a professional. People with a history of trauma may want guidance from a trained provider.

NCCIH also says mindfulness practices should not replace conventional medical care or delay seeing a health care provider. If you are in treatment, keep going and mention to your provider that you are trying mindfulness.

## Mindfulness and real life

Mindfulness cannot fix a stressful job, an unsafe home, or money problems. It may help you cope with how those things feel, but it does not remove the cause. If your stress is mostly from your circumstances, practical changes and support matter too.

## Getting started

Here are some simple ways to begin:

- Take three slow breaths before opening your phone in the morning.
- Spend a minute noticing what you can see, hear, and feel right now.
- Eat one bite or sip with full attention.
- Try a short walk without headphones, noticing the air and your steps.
- Ask about the training of any instructor or app you try, as NCCIH suggests.

**Example (fictional):** Ken tries sitting for ten minutes and feels restless and frustrated. He switches to two minutes at the kitchen table with his tea, noticing the warmth of the cup. After a week, he finds he can sometimes catch himself getting wound up in traffic. It is a small change, and it is his own pace.

Mindfulness can be a helpful tool for some people. It is one option among many, and it works best as part of a wider plan for your wellbeing.', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["Mindfulness means noticing the present moment without judging it. Your mind wandering is normal.","Research suggests benefits for stress, anxiety, depression, and sleep for some people, but much of it is preliminary.","About 8% of participants in one review reported negative effects, so go gently and stop if it makes you feel worse.","Mindfulness should not replace medical or mental health care.","Short, simple practices count. You do not need long sessions."]'::jsonb, '["When during my day could I take one mindful minute?","What happens in my body when I slow down and pay attention?","What would make a mindfulness practice feel safe and doable for me?"]'::jsonb, '**The five-senses pause**

This takes about two minutes and can be done anywhere.

1. Stop and take a slow breath.
2. Notice **5** things you can see.
3. Notice **4** things you can feel, such as your clothes, the chair, or the air on your skin.
4. Notice **3** things you can hear.
5. Notice **2** things you can smell, or two things you like the smell of.
6. Notice **1** thing you can taste, or take a sip of water.
7. Take one more slow breath.

If your mind wanders, simply return to the next sense. If this exercise makes you feel more anxious or brings up difficult memories, stop and do something that feels grounding. It is a stress-relief skill, not a replacement for treatment.', '[{"title":"Meditation and Mindfulness: Effectiveness and Safety","publisher":"National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NIH)","year":"2022","url":"https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/meditation-and-mindfulness-effectiveness-and-safety"},{"title":"Mindfulness meditation: A research-proven way to reduce stress","publisher":"American Psychological Association","year":"2019","url":"https://www.apa.org/topics/mindfulness/meditation"}]'::jsonb, '["emotional-regulation-skills","understanding-stress","sleep-routines-that-help"]'::jsonb, array['mental wellness','article']::text[], false, '', 'If mindfulness makes you feel worse, or if you have symptoms of depression, anxiety, or trauma that interfere with life, talk with a licensed mental health professional or your doctor. Do not use mindfulness in place of care you have been prescribed. If you are in crisis or thinking about suicide, call or text 988 (the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) or chat at chat.988lifeline.org, any time, in the U.S. For a life-threatening emergency, call 911.', 'Much mindfulness research is early-stage, uses many different methods, and has few long-term follow-ups. Benefits seen in studies may not apply to everyone.', 4, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('sleep-routines-that-help', 'sleep-routines', 'wellness', 'article', 'Sleep Routines That Help: Small Habits for Better Rest', 'Sleep and mood are closely linked. This article covers how much sleep adults need and practical habits from public health agencies.', 'Sleep and mental health are closely connected. When you are tired, everything can feel harder. When you are stressed or low, sleep can slip away. This article offers general information and gentle habits. It is not medical advice, and it cannot tell you whether you have a sleep disorder.

## How much sleep do adults need?

The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) reports that adults ages 18 to 60 need 7 or more hours of sleep per day, adults 61 to 64 need 7 to 9 hours, and adults 65 and older need 7 to 8 hours. The CDC attributes these numbers to consensus recommendations from the National Sleep Foundation, the American Academy of Sleep Medicine, and the Sleep Research Society. The CDC also says quality matters as much as quantity, and describes quality sleep as uninterrupted and refreshing.

## Why a routine helps

The body likes rhythm. Going to bed and waking up at about the same time each day helps your body know when to feel sleepy and when to feel alert. The National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute (NHLBI) suggests keeping weeknight and weekend schedules within about an hour of each other.

## Habits that may help

The CDC and NHLBI list many practical tips:

- **Keep a regular schedule.** Try to go to bed and get up at similar times every day.
- **Make your room restful.** Aim for quiet, cool, and dark. NHLBI says a dim night light is okay.
- **Wind down.** NHLBI suggests spending the hour before bed on calm activities. A warm bath or relaxation techniques can help.
- **Put screens away.** The CDC suggests turning off electronic devices at least 30 minutes before bed. NHLBI notes bright screens can signal your brain to stay alert.
- **Watch caffeine.** NHLBI says caffeine''s effects can last up to 8 hours, so a late-afternoon coffee may affect your sleep. The CDC suggests limiting caffeine in the afternoon and evening.
- **Skip big meals and alcohol late.** Both the CDC and NHLBI suggest avoiding heavy meals and alcohol close to bedtime.
- **Be active in the day.** Daytime movement and outdoor time may help. Avoid intense exercise right before bed.
- **Keep naps short.** NHLBI suggests limiting naps to about 20 minutes for adults, or taking them earlier in the afternoon.

You do not have to do all of these. Pick one or two that fit your life and give them a few weeks.

## When life makes sleep hard

Advice like "just go to bed on time" does not work for everyone. Shift work, caring for a baby or a sick relative, noisy or unsafe housing, a second job, pain, or worries about money can all get in the way. NHLBI offers tips for shift workers, such as keeping work lighting bright, limiting shift changes, using light-blocking curtains for daytime sleep, and having caffeine only early in a shift. If you cannot control your schedule, focus on what you can: a dark room, a wind-down ritual, or protecting one stretch of sleep.

## Sleep, stress, and mood

Stress and low mood can make sleep worse, and poor sleep can make stress and low mood feel worse. The National Institute of Mental Health (NIMH) lists trouble sleeping among the symptoms that, if they last 2 weeks or more along with other distress, are a reason to talk to a professional. Sleep changes are also common in anxiety and depression.

**Example (fictional):** Rosa works nights at a hospital and sleeps during the day. She hangs blackout curtains, uses a fan for noise, and keeps the same sleep window on her days off as much as she can. She still has rough weeks, but she feels less foggy. When she notices she is also feeling very low, she mentions both to her doctor.

## When to get help

If you have trouble sleeping most nights for weeks, snore loudly and wake up gasping, feel very sleepy during the day, or if sleep problems come with low mood or anxiety, talk to a health care provider. Sleep problems are common and treatable, and a provider can look for causes. Do not start or stop any sleep medication without talking to your doctor.

Better sleep rarely happens overnight. Small, steady habits and patience with yourself can help.', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["The CDC reports adults ages 18 to 60 need 7 or more hours of sleep. Quality matters too.","A steady wake-up and bedtime routine helps the body know when to rest.","Dark, quiet, cool rooms, limiting late caffeine, and winding down without screens are common tips.","Life situations like shift work and caregiving can make sleep hard. Focus on what you can control.","Ongoing sleep trouble is worth discussing with a health care provider."]'::jsonb, '["What gets in the way of my sleep, and which of those things can I change?","What is one small wind-down habit I could try this week?","How does my mood change after a bad night''s sleep?"]'::jsonb, '**A simple wind-down**

Try this for 10 to 15 minutes before bed, for a week.

1. Choose a time to start winding down and set a quiet reminder.
2. Dim the lights and put screens away, or use them only for something calm.
3. Do one gentle thing: take a warm shower, stretch, read something light, or listen to quiet music.
4. Jot down any worries or tomorrow''s to-do list on paper to get them out of your head.
5. Get into bed and take five slow breaths.

If you are awake after what feels like 20 minutes, it can help to get up and do something calm in low light until you feel sleepy. This is general sleep hygiene and does not replace care for a sleep disorder.', '[{"title":"About Sleep","publisher":"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention","year":"2024","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/sleep/about/index.html"},{"title":"Healthy Sleep Habits","publisher":"National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute (NIH)","year":"2022","url":"https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep-deprivation/healthy-sleep-habits"},{"title":"Caring for Your Mental Health","publisher":"National Institute of Mental Health","year":"n.d.","url":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/topics/caring-for-your-mental-health"}]'::jsonb, '["understanding-stress","mindfulness-basics","burnout-signs-and-recovery"]'::jsonb, array['mental wellness','article']::text[], false, '', 'If sleep problems last for weeks, affect your day, or come with loud snoring, gasping, low mood, or anxiety, talk with your doctor or another licensed health professional. Do not start or stop sleep medication without medical advice. If you are in crisis or thinking about suicide, call or text 988 (the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) or chat at chat.988lifeline.org, any time, in the U.S. For a life-threatening emergency, call 911.', 'These are general recommendations from public health agencies. Sleep needs vary by person, and tips that help one person may not help another.', 4, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('grief-and-loss', 'grief-loss', 'wellness', 'article', 'Grief and Loss: Living With a Heavy Heart', 'Grief is a natural response to loss and looks different for everyone. Learn what is common, what can help, and when more support may be needed.', 'Grief is what we feel when we lose someone or something that matters to us. It may follow a death, but it can also follow other losses, like a relationship ending, a health change, or a home or job lost. There is no right way to grieve. This article offers general information and cannot replace support from people who know you or from a professional.

## What grief can look like

The American Psychological Association (APA) defines grief as the anguish experienced after significant loss, usually the death of a beloved person. It notes that grief responses differ from person to person, and that not all grief is given public expression. The National Cancer Institute (NCI) says "there is no typical grief response."

According to the NCI, common reactions include numbness, disbelief, anxiety about being separated from the person, sadness, anger, changes in sleep and appetite, and fatigue. Some people also feel moments of acceptance or even relief. All of these can be part of grief. You might cry a lot, or hardly at all. You might feel fine one day and flattened the next.

## Grief comes in waves

The NCI describes grief as sometimes coming in short, intense bursts, often set off by reminders such as holidays, anniversaries, or a favorite song. This can catch people off guard, even long after the loss. The NCI notes that for most people, symptoms ease between 6 months and 2 years after the loss. Those are general patterns and not deadlines. Your timeline is your own.

## Grief and depression

Grief and depression can look alike, but they are not the same. The NCI notes that in normal grief, painful feelings tend to come and go, and self-esteem is usually preserved. In major depression, low mood tends to be more constant. If you are not sure which you are experiencing, a health care provider can help sort it out.

## What can help

The NCI says most people work through grief without treatment, and that support from family, friends, and community is important. Ideas to consider:

- **Lean on people.** Let trusted friends or family help with meals, errands, or just sitting with you.
- **Take care of the basics.** Rest, eat, and move when you can, even in small ways.
- **Use rituals that matter to you.** Faith, culture, memorials, or remembering a person in your own way can be comforting for some people. The NCI notes research on the benefits of religion and cultural practices is mixed, so follow what feels right for you.
- **Talk about the person.** Sharing stories can keep a connection alive.
- **Be gentle with your calendar.** Big decisions can often wait.
- **Find a group.** Many communities and hospices offer grief groups.

Grief is also shaped by what is happening around you. Money stress, caring for others, unfair or traumatic circumstances around a death, or losing support can make grief heavier. Needing extra help in these situations is reasonable.

## When grief may need extra support

The NCI describes complicated grief as grief that does not improve over a long period, causes extreme distress, affects many areas of life, and reduces your ability to carry out daily activities. It notes that people with limited social support are more at risk. The NCI says cognitive behavioral therapy can help people with complicated grief, and that talking with someone trained in grief and bereavement can help. The APA also notes that intense grief can become life-threatening through disruption of the immune system, self-neglect, and suicidal thoughts. If you are having thoughts of ending your life, please call or text 988 right away.

**Example (fictional):** After her father dies, Amara feels numb for weeks, then breaks down in a grocery store when she sees his favorite cereal. A friend sits with her in the car. Later, Amara joins a bereavement group at a local hospice, where she hears others describe the same waves. A year on, the sadness is still there, but it has softened. She still has hard days around his birthday, and she plans for them.

Missing someone is a sign of love. There is nothing wrong with you for hurting.', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["Grief is a natural response to loss, and there is no single right way to grieve.","Grief often comes in waves, and reminders can bring feelings back.","Support from family, friends, and community matters. Most people work through grief with that support.","Grief that stays extremely distressing for a long time, or disrupts daily life, is worth discussing with a professional.","If you are having thoughts of suicide, call or text 988 or call 911 in an emergency."]'::jsonb, '["Who or what have I lost, and what do I miss most?","What helps me feel even a little supported right now?","Is there someone I could let in a bit more?"]'::jsonb, '**A small remembrance**

Go at your own pace. You can stop at any time.

1. Find a quiet moment. Take a slow breath.
2. Think of one memory of the person or thing you lost that brings a bit of warmth, even if it also brings sadness.
3. Write it down in a few sentences, or say it aloud, or tell it to someone you trust.
4. Notice what comes up in your body, without trying to change it.
5. Finish by doing something kind for yourself, like a drink of water, a walk, or a call with a friend.

If this is too painful right now, set it aside. That is okay. This exercise is a gentle way to honor your loss and is not a substitute for grief counseling or other professional support.', '[{"title":"Grief, Bereavement, and Loss (PDQ) - Patient Version","publisher":"National Cancer Institute (NIH)","year":"2025","url":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/advanced-cancer/caregivers/planning/bereavement-pdq"},{"title":"Grief","publisher":"American Psychological Association","year":"n.d.","url":"https://www.apa.org/topics/grief"}]'::jsonb, '["understanding-depression","understanding-loneliness","seeking-professional-help"]'::jsonb, array['mental wellness','article']::text[], true, 'This article discusses grief, death, and mentions suicide. Please take care while reading.', 'If grief feels unbearable, does not ease over a long time, or keeps you from daily life, talk with your doctor or a licensed mental health professional, or ask about grief counseling through a hospice or community group. If you are in crisis or thinking about suicide, call or text 988 (the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) or chat at chat.988lifeline.org, any time, in the U.S. For a life-threatening emergency, call 911.', 'This is general education. Grief varies widely by person, culture, and circumstance, and research on what helps is still developing.', 4, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('building-healthy-relationships', 'healthy-relationships', 'wellness', 'article', 'Building Healthy Relationships: Respect, Trust, and Care', 'Healthy relationships support our wellbeing. Learn what they tend to look like, how to keep them strong, and where to turn if a relationship is unsafe.', 'The people in our lives can make a big difference to how we feel. Strong, safe relationships can be a source of comfort and support. This article looks at what healthy relationships tend to include, what research says about connection, and what to do if a relationship does not feel safe. It applies to partners, friends, family, and coworkers, though some details differ.

## Why relationships matter

The U.S. Surgeon General''s 2023 advisory on loneliness and isolation reports that social connection is linked with better health. It cites findings that across 148 studies, social connection increased the odds of survival by 50%. It also reports that poor social relationships were associated with a 29% higher risk of heart disease and a 32% higher risk of stroke. These are associations from large studies and do not prove cause, but they show how much relationships can matter.

## What a healthy relationship tends to look like

The National Domestic Violence Hotline explains that relationships exist on a spectrum from healthy to abusive, with unhealthy in between. In a healthy relationship, partners generally:

- Communicate openly and listen to each other.
- Respect each other''s opinions, even when they differ.
- Trust each other without needing proof.
- Are honest while still having some privacy.
- Share decisions fairly.
- Respect each other''s need for time and space, alone or with other people.
- Make sure everyone consents to sexual activity.

The same ideas apply in friendships and family life. Respect, honesty, and safety matter everywhere.

## Habits that keep relationships strong

The American Psychological Association (APA) offers advice for couples that applies more widely:

- **Check in regularly.** Spend a few minutes talking about more than logistics.
- **Bring up hard topics kindly.** Avoiding issues can build resentment.
- **Handle conflict with care.** The APA notes that yelling, personal attacks, and withdrawing from discussions are damaging. Listening to the other person''s view and acknowledging their feelings is healthier.
- **Keep things fresh.** Trying new things together can help.
- **Get help when you are stuck.** Repeating the same argument is a warning sign, and psychologists can help people improve communication.

The U.S. Surgeon General''s advisory also suggests responding to calls from loved ones, sharing meals, and putting your phone away during conversations so you can fully listen.

## Real life gets in the way

Time, money, work schedules, caregiving, health problems, and past hurts can all strain relationships. Having tension in hard times does not mean a relationship is failing. Honest conversations, small gestures, and patience matter. It is also fine for relationships to change over time. Some need more distance, and some end.

## When a relationship is not safe

Healthy relationships do not include fear, control, threats, or violence. The Hotline notes that if you need support, you can call 800.799.SAFE (7233), text START to 88788, or chat with an advocate. If you are in immediate danger, call 911. Wanting to leave or to get support is reasonable. You are not to blame for another person''s harmful behavior.

**Example (fictional):** Chris and Alex realize their evenings are mostly spent on chores and screens. They agree on ten minutes each night to talk about one thing that has nothing to do with chores. At first it feels awkward. After a few weeks, they find they are laughing more and snapping less. When they disagree about money, they use the same habit: each person speaks, the other repeats back what they heard, and then they swap.

Relationships take effort from everyone involved. Small, regular acts of care add up, and it is never too late to start.', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["Social connection is linked to better health in large studies, though these are associations and not proof of cause.","Healthy relationships include open communication, respect, trust, honesty, shared decisions, and consent.","Regular check-ins, kind handling of conflict, and listening can strengthen connection.","Relationships can be strained by outside pressures. That does not mean you have failed.","If a relationship involves fear, control, or violence, support is available through the National Domestic Violence Hotline at 800.799.SAFE (7233) or 911 in an emergency."]'::jsonb, '["Which relationships give me energy, and which drain it?","What is one small way I could show care for someone this week?","Is there a topic I have been avoiding that I might raise kindly?"]'::jsonb, '**A relationship check-in**

Take 5 to 10 minutes alone with a pen and paper.

1. Pick one relationship you want to look at.
2. Write down three things that go well in it.
3. Write down one thing you would like to be different.
4. Ask yourself: *Do I feel safe, respected, and able to be myself here?*
5. Pick one small step, such as saying thanks, setting a time to talk, or asking for something you need.

If you notice fear, control, or harm in a relationship, consider reaching out to a trusted person or the National Domestic Violence Hotline (800.799.SAFE). This is a reflection tool and is not a replacement for counseling.', '[{"title":"Happy couples: How to keep your relationship healthy","publisher":"American Psychological Association","year":"2020","url":"https://www.apa.org/topics/healthy-relationships"},{"title":"Healthy Relationships","publisher":"The National Domestic Violence Hotline","year":"n.d.","url":"https://www.thehotline.org/resources/healthy-relationships/"},{"title":"Our Epidemic of Loneliness and Isolation: The U.S. Surgeon General''s Advisory on the Healing Effects of Social Connection and Community","publisher":"U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Office of the Surgeon General","year":"2023","url":"https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/surgeon-general-social-connection-advisory.pdf"}]'::jsonb, '["communication-and-boundaries","understanding-loneliness","seeking-professional-help"]'::jsonb, array['mental wellness','article']::text[], false, '', 'If relationship problems are causing ongoing distress, couples or family counseling with a licensed professional may help. If you feel unsafe, call the National Domestic Violence Hotline at 800.799.SAFE (7233) or 911 in an emergency. If you are in crisis or thinking about suicide, call or text 988 (the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) or chat at chat.988lifeline.org, any time, in the U.S. For a life-threatening emergency, call 911.', 'Studies linking relationships to health show associations, not proof that relationships cause better health. Advice here is general and may not fit every culture, relationship type, or situation.', 4, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('communication-and-boundaries', 'communication-boundaries', 'wellness', 'article', 'Communication and Boundaries: Saying What You Need', 'Boundaries are limits that help you stay safe and respected. Learn how to express them clearly and kindly.', 'Many people find it hard to say no, ask for what they need, or tell someone that a line has been crossed. You may worry about conflict, being seen as rude, or letting people down. Communication and boundaries are skills, and skills can be learned. This article offers general ideas, not a script for every situation.

## What boundaries are

University of Illinois Extension describes boundaries as guidelines or limits a person creates to decide what behavior from others is safe and respectful, and how they will respond when those limits are crossed. The University of Kansas Counseling and Psychological Services (CAPS) describes boundaries in several areas, including physical (personal space, privacy, unwanted touch) and emotional (stating your emotional needs so you don''t become a people-pleaser or get overly affected by others'' moods).

Boundaries can also cover time, money, work, and technology. They look different from person to person, and they can change over time.

## Why they can be hard

The Kansas CAPS page notes that fear of rejection or conflict can lead people to agree when they don''t want to, which can build helplessness, depression, and resentment. Negative beliefs about yourself, like feeling unworthy, can also get in the way. And some people never learned how to set boundaries, because no one showed them.

Culture, family, and power also play a part. It can be much harder to set a limit with a boss, a relative you depend on, or someone who becomes angry when you say no. You know your situation best. It is okay to choose when and how to speak up, and your safety comes first.

## Boundaries and assertive communication

Kansas CAPS explains that boundary setting is part of assertive behavior: "assertive behavior is about controlling yourself, not other people." Assertive communication means stating your wants and needs without blaming. That is different from being passive (staying silent) or aggressive (trying to control or put down the other person).

University of Illinois Extension suggests stating the boundary clearly, calmly, firmly, and respectfully, using as few words as possible. You do not need to justify or apologize for it. You are responsible for how you communicate the boundary, not for how the other person reacts.

## Simple ways to say it

- **Use "I" statements.** "I need some quiet time tonight," instead of "You never leave me alone."
- **Be specific.** "I can''t take on another project this week" is clearer than "I''m so busy."
- **Keep it short.** Long explanations can invite debate.
- **Say what you can do.** "I can''t talk now, but I can call you tomorrow at 6."
- **Stay calm and repeat.** If someone pushes back, you can calmly repeat the boundary.
- **Follow through.** A boundary means something only if you act on it, such as leaving a conversation that turns abusive.

The American Psychological Association (APA) also notes that healthy couples check in regularly, listen to each other''s perspective, and acknowledge feelings. Good communication is a two-way street. It includes listening as well as speaking.

## When others do not respect them

Sometimes people push back, sulk, or ignore a boundary, especially when it is new. Early discomfort does not necessarily mean you did something wrong. But if someone repeatedly crosses your limits, threatens you, or frightens you, it may be a sign of an unhealthy relationship. The National Domestic Violence Hotline can be reached at 800.799.SAFE (7233), and in an emergency, call 911.

**Example (fictional):** Maya''s coworker often asks her to cover his shift at the last minute. She feels guilty saying no, so she usually says yes and then feels angry. This time she says, "I can''t cover this one. I can help find someone else if you''d like." He grumbles but finds another person. Maya''s heart pounds, but she feels lighter afterward.

## Give yourself time

If setting boundaries feels scary, start with small, low-risk ones. Practice with someone you trust. A counselor can also help you rehearse, especially if past experiences make speaking up feel unsafe.', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["Boundaries are limits that help you decide what treatment from others is safe and respectful.","Assertive communication states your needs without blaming or controlling others.","Short, clear, calm statements usually work better than long explanations.","You are responsible for how you share a boundary, not for how another person reacts.","If a boundary is met with threats or fear, your safety comes first. Support is available."]'::jsonb, '["Where in my life do I say yes when I mean no?","What is one boundary I want to set, and what is the simplest way to say it?","Who could I practice a hard conversation with?"]'::jsonb, '**Practice one boundary sentence**

1. Think of a low-stakes situation where you would like to set a limit, such as declining an extra task or asking for quiet time.
2. Fill in this frame: *"I can''t ___. I can ___ instead."* or *"I need ___."*
3. Say it out loud once or twice, or write it down. Keep it short, with no big apology.
4. Decide in advance what you will do if the person pushes back, such as repeating the sentence calmly.
5. Try it with someone safe, and notice how you feel afterward.

Skip this if the other person might react in a way that is unsafe. In that case, think about getting support first. This exercise does not replace counseling or safety planning.', '[{"title":"Setting Boundaries","publisher":"University of Illinois Extension","year":"n.d.","url":"https://extension.illinois.edu/everyones-mental-health-matters/setting-boundaries"},{"title":"Setting Boundaries","publisher":"Counseling and Psychological Services, University of Kansas","year":"n.d.","url":"https://caps.ku.edu/setting-boundaries"},{"title":"Happy couples: How to keep your relationship healthy","publisher":"American Psychological Association","year":"2020","url":"https://www.apa.org/topics/healthy-relationships"}]'::jsonb, '["building-healthy-relationships","self-compassion-basics","burnout-signs-and-recovery"]'::jsonb, array['mental wellness','article']::text[], false, '', 'If you find it very hard to speak up, or if a relationship involves fear, control, or violence, consider talking with a licensed counselor or therapist. For domestic violence support, call the National Domestic Violence Hotline at 800.799.SAFE (7233). If you are in crisis or thinking about suicide, call or text 988 (the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) or chat at chat.988lifeline.org, any time, in the U.S. For a life-threatening emergency, call 911.', 'Guidance here comes from university counseling resources and an APA article, which offer general education and not research proof. What works depends on culture, safety, and the relationship.', 4, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('understanding-loneliness', 'loneliness-connection', 'wellness', 'article', 'Loneliness and Connection: Why We Need Each Other', 'Loneliness is common and it is not a personal failing. Learn what research says and small, realistic ways to build connection.', 'Almost everyone feels lonely at some point. You can feel lonely in a crowd, in a relationship, or after moving to a new place. Loneliness is a signal that we need connection, much like hunger is a signal that we need food. This article explains what research says about loneliness and offers small steps that may help.

## How common is it?

In 2023, the U.S. Surgeon General issued an advisory called "Our Epidemic of Loneliness and Isolation." It reports that approximately half of U.S. adults report experiencing loneliness, with some of the highest rates among young adults. It also reports that a 2022 study found only 39% of U.S. adults said they felt very connected to others emotionally. The advisory notes that fewer than 20% of people who often or always feel lonely or isolated recognize it as a major problem.

So if you feel lonely, you are far from alone in that.

## Why connection matters for health

The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) says that strong social connections are linked with a lower risk of heart disease, stroke, dementia, depression, and anxiety. It also says connection can improve stress management and sleep quality. The Surgeon General''s advisory reports that loneliness and social isolation are associated with a higher risk of premature death (26% and 29%, respectively), and says lacking social connection can increase the risk for premature death as much as smoking up to 15 cigarettes a day.

These are large-scale associations. They do not mean that every lonely person will become ill, and they do not prove that loneliness is the direct cause. They do suggest that connection is an important part of health, not just a nice extra.

## Loneliness and isolation are not the same

Loneliness is the feeling of being alone or disconnected. Social isolation is having few relationships or contacts. You can be isolated without feeling lonely, and lonely with plenty of people around. Both can matter.

## Real barriers

It is not always easy to "just get out there." Moving, job changes, caring for others, health or mobility problems, disability, cost, transportation, discrimination, and time can all reduce connection. Rural areas and busy lives can make it harder. The Surgeon General''s advisory notes that time spent alone has been rising. It reports that the average time spent alone increased from 285 minutes per day in 2003 to 333 minutes per day in 2020. If you feel disconnected, that may reflect real changes around you and not something wrong with you.

## Small steps toward connection

The CDC and the Surgeon General suggest several ideas:

- **Respond.** Reply to a call or message from a friend or loved one.
- **Share a meal.** Eating with others, even occasionally, can build closeness.
- **Be fully present.** Put your phone away during a conversation.
- **Use shared spaces.** The CDC suggests places like neighborhoods, schools, places of worship, workplaces, and parks.
- **Give back.** Volunteering or helping others can strengthen bonds.
- **Be honest.** Sharing your thoughts and feelings openly can deepen relationships.

You do not need many friends. A few reliable people can make a difference. Online connection can help too, especially if it leads to real-life contact or fits your needs, but it may not feel the same for everyone.

**Example (fictional):** Tom retires and moves to a new town. After a few months, he realizes he has barely spoken to anyone. He starts by saying hello to the same barista each morning. Later he joins a weekly walking group at the library. He doesn''t click with everyone, but he looks forward to Thursdays. It took time, and it started small.

## When loneliness is heavy

Long-lasting loneliness can go along with depression or anxiety. If loneliness feels heavy, hopeless, or constant, talk with a health care provider or a licensed mental health professional. If you ever think about harming yourself, call or text 988 right away.

Connection is a basic human need, and wanting it is nothing to be ashamed of.', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["The Surgeon General''s advisory reports that about half of U.S. adults report experiencing loneliness.","Loneliness is a feeling, and social isolation is having few contacts. Either can affect health.","Barriers like caregiving, cost, health, and discrimination are real. Loneliness is not a personal failure.","Small steps like replying to a message, sharing a meal, or joining a group can build connection.","If loneliness is heavy or constant, a professional can help."]'::jsonb, '["When do I feel most connected, and what is happening then?","Who is one person I could reach out to this week?","What small barrier is in my way, and could I get help with it?"]'::jsonb, '**A connection map**

1. On paper, draw a small circle for yourself in the middle.
2. Around it, write the names of people, groups, or places that give you any sense of connection, even a little: a neighbor, a coworker, a faith group, an online forum, a pet.
3. Draw a line to the ones that feel closest, and a dotted line to ones you would like to build.
4. Pick one dotted line. Write one tiny action you could take this week, such as sending a text, saying hello, or looking up a local group.
5. Do that one action, and notice how it feels.

If your map is nearly empty, that is not a verdict on you. It is a starting point. This exercise does not replace professional support if you are feeling very low.', '[{"title":"Our Epidemic of Loneliness and Isolation: The U.S. Surgeon General''s Advisory on the Healing Effects of Social Connection and Community","publisher":"U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Office of the Surgeon General","year":"2023","url":"https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/surgeon-general-social-connection-advisory.pdf"},{"title":"Social Connection","publisher":"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention","year":"n.d.","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/about/index.html"}]'::jsonb, '["building-healthy-relationships","understanding-depression","building-resilience"]'::jsonb, array['mental wellness','article']::text[], false, '', 'If loneliness feels heavy, constant, or comes with low mood or anxiety, talk with a doctor or licensed mental health professional. SAMHSA''s National Helpline (1-800-662-4357) and findtreatment.gov can help you find services. If you are in crisis or thinking about suicide, call or text 988 (the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) or chat at chat.988lifeline.org, any time, in the U.S. For a life-threatening emergency, call 911.', 'Studies on loneliness and health show associations, and it can be hard to separate cause from effect. Survey numbers depend on how loneliness was measured.', 4, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('burnout-signs-and-recovery', 'burnout', 'wellness', 'article', 'Burnout: Signs, Causes, and Ways to Recover', 'Burnout comes from long-term stress at work that hasn''t been managed. Learn what the World Health Organization says it is and what may help.', 'Do you feel drained before the workday even begins? Do you feel more distant from your work than you used to, or like nothing you do makes a difference? You might be dealing with burnout. This article explains what burnout is, what contributes to it, and what may help. It is general information and cannot diagnose burnout or any other condition.

## What burnout is

The World Health Organization (WHO) includes burn-out in the International Classification of Diseases as an "occupational phenomenon." It defines burn-out as a syndrome conceptualized as resulting from chronic workplace stress that has not been successfully managed. The WHO says it is characterized by three dimensions:

1. Feelings of energy depletion or exhaustion.
2. Increased mental distance from one''s job, or feelings of negativism or cynicism related to one''s job.
3. Reduced professional efficacy.

The WHO adds that burn-out refers specifically to the occupational context and should not be applied to describe experiences in other areas of life. In everyday conversation, people also use the word for exhaustion from caregiving, school, or parenting. Those experiences are real and deserve care, even though the WHO''s definition applies to work.

## It is not a personal weakness

Burnout is often described as a person problem, but the WHO links it to workplace stress. The Office of the U.S. Surgeon General''s workplace framework points to conditions at work, such as protection from harm, connection and community, work-life harmony, mattering at work, and opportunity for growth, as important for worker wellbeing. The Surgeon General''s workplace page also notes that fatigue reduces productivity and that the risk of burnout rises as fatigue grows, and that limited access to paid leave can contribute to burnout.

Heavy workloads, unfair treatment, low control, long hours, unclear expectations, and little recognition can all play a part. So can caring for others on top of a job. If your situation is the problem, then rest alone may not be enough. Change may be needed in the workplace too.

## Signs to notice

Along with the three WHO dimensions, you might notice trouble sleeping, getting sick more often, dreading work, irritability, trouble concentrating, or pulling away from people. Because these signs can also be linked to depression or anxiety, it helps to talk with a health professional rather than guess.

## What may help

There is no single fix. The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) offers general tips for coping with stress that may help:

- **Protect your sleep.** The CDC says adults need 7 or more hours.
- **Move your body** in ways that feel doable.
- **Take real breaks.** Time outdoors, hobbies, and relaxation matter.
- **Limit news and social media** if they add to your stress.
- **Stay connected** with trusted people.

At work, you might try setting limits on after-hours messages, asking for help with workload, talking with a manager or human resources about changes, using paid time off if you have it, or asking whether your employer offers an Employee Assistance Program (EAP). The National Institute of Mental Health describes an EAP as a free and confidential service. Not everyone can change jobs or take time off, and that is a real constraint. Even small shifts, like a boundary around lunch, can help.

**Example (fictional):** Nia, a nurse, notices she has started to feel numb about patients and dreads her shifts. She talks to her supervisor about her schedule and asks about the hospital''s EAP. She also starts protecting two evenings a week for rest. The job is still demanding. But she feels less alone, and she gets a referral to counseling to talk through what she has been carrying.

## When to get support

If exhaustion, hopelessness, or loss of interest lasts for weeks, or if it affects your health or safety, please talk to a doctor or a licensed mental health professional. They can help figure out whether burnout, depression, or something else may be involved.', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["The WHO defines burn-out as a syndrome from chronic workplace stress that has not been successfully managed, with exhaustion, distance from the job, and reduced efficacy.","Burnout is tied to workplace conditions, not personal weakness.","Sleep, movement, breaks, and connection can help, but may not be enough if the workplace does not change.","Options at work can include limits on hours, asking for help, paid leave, or an Employee Assistance Program.","If symptoms last for weeks or affect your health, see a health professional."]'::jsonb, '["Which parts of my job give me energy, and which drain it?","What is one limit I could set or ask for at work this week?","Who could I talk to about how I have been feeling?"]'::jsonb, '**An energy audit**

1. Make two lists on paper: **What drains me** and **What restores me**. Think about work, home, and relationships.
2. Circle one item from the drain list that you have some influence over (for example, a recurring meeting or checking messages at night).
3. Circle one item from the restore list that you could add or protect this week (a walk, a call with a friend, a full lunch break).
4. Write one small action for each circle, and put it on your calendar.
5. After a week, notice what shifted.

You may not be able to change everything on your drain list, and that is not your fault. If you feel overwhelmed or hopeless, please speak with a professional. This exercise is not a substitute for care.', '[{"title":"Burn-out an \"occupational phenomenon\": International Classification of Diseases","publisher":"World Health Organization","year":"2019","url":"https://www.who.int/news/item/28-05-2019-burn-out-an-occupational-phenomenon-international-classification-of-diseases"},{"title":"Workplace Mental Health & Well-Being","publisher":"U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Office of the Surgeon General","year":"2025","url":"https://www.hhs.gov/surgeongeneral/priorities/workplace-well-being/index.html"},{"title":"Managing Stress","publisher":"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention","year":"n.d.","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/mental-health/living-with/index.html"},{"title":"Help for Mental Illnesses","publisher":"National Institute of Mental Health","year":"n.d.","url":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/find-help"}]'::jsonb, '["understanding-stress","communication-and-boundaries","sleep-routines-that-help"]'::jsonb, array['mental wellness','article']::text[], false, '', 'If you feel exhausted, hopeless, or numb for weeks, talk with your doctor or a licensed mental health professional, since burnout can overlap with depression or anxiety. Ask if your employer offers an Employee Assistance Program. If you are in crisis or thinking about suicide, call or text 988 (the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) or chat at chat.988lifeline.org, any time, in the U.S. For a life-threatening emergency, call 911.', 'Burnout is not a medical diagnosis in the same way as depression. Definitions vary, and research on what works for recovery is still developing.', 4, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('building-resilience', 'resilience', 'wellness', 'article', 'Building Resilience: Adapting Through Hard Times', 'Resilience is not about being tough or never struggling. It is the process of adapting to hardship, and it can be built over time.', 'When life gets hard, people sometimes say, "Be strong" or "Stay positive." But resilience is not about pretending you are fine. This article looks at what resilience really means and how people build it, while recognizing that some hardships are too big for any one person to handle alone.

## What resilience is

The American Psychological Association (APA) describes resilience as the process of adapting well in the face of adversity. It includes bouncing back from hard experiences and can also involve personal growth. The APA stresses that resilience does not mean a lack of distress. People who go through major adversity often feel emotional pain, and the path to resilience can involve considerable distress.

The APA also says resilience is not a fixed trait that only some people have. It involves behaviors, thoughts, and actions that anyone can learn and develop.

## Resilience is shaped by circumstances

Individual effort matters, but it is not the whole story. The World Health Organization (WHO) notes that social and environmental factors such as poverty, violence, and inequality raise the risk of mental health problems, while protective factors include social and emotional skills, positive social interactions, access to education and decent work, safe neighborhoods, and strong community ties. In other words, resilience grows when there are resources and support around a person. If you have been struggling without those supports, it is not a lack of toughness.

## Things that can help

The APA describes four broad areas:

- **Connection.** Keep relationships with supportive people. Consider community, faith-based, or civic groups.
- **Wellness.** Care for your body with sleep, nutrition, exercise, and hydration. Practices like journaling, meditation, or prayer can help. The APA discourages using substances to cope.
- **Purpose.** Helping others, taking small steps on problems, and setting realistic goals can give a sense of direction.
- **Healthy thinking.** Challenge catastrophic or overly negative thoughts, accept changes you cannot control, and draw on lessons from past difficulties.

You do not need to do everything. Choose what fits your life, culture, and energy.

The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) adds practical stress-management ideas, such as limiting news and social media, staying active, keeping a consistent sleep schedule, and staying connected with trusted people.

## Resilience is not going it alone

Asking for help is part of resilience. The APA says that getting help when you need it is crucial, and that many people can build resilience on their own but some get stuck. A licensed mental health professional can help you work out a plan, especially if a stressful experience keeps you from functioning as well as you would like or from handling basic daily activities.

## Hopeful, not forced

Healthy thinking does not mean forced positivity. You can admit that something is unfair and painful and still look for small steps. Hope can sit beside grief, anger, and fear.

**Example (fictional):** After losing his job, Omar feels ashamed and avoids his friends. He admits to himself that he is scared. He tells one friend, who connects him to a job-search group. He also starts a short daily walk and keeps a regular wake-up time. He still has bad days, but the group gives him structure and hope. He also sees his doctor about trouble sleeping.

## Building it over time

Resilience grows through everyday practice and support. Small steps count, such as checking in with a friend, getting outside, or noting one thing that went okay. Over time, those steps can add up.', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["Resilience is the process of adapting to hardship, and it does not mean avoiding pain or distress.","It can be learned. It is not a fixed trait that only some people have.","Supportive relationships, caring for your body, purpose, and healthy thinking are common building blocks.","Circumstances matter. Safe communities and resources support resilience.","Getting professional help when you need it is part of being resilient."]'::jsonb, '["What has helped me get through a hard time before?","Who is in my corner, and who could I add?","What is one small thing that gives me a sense of purpose right now?"]'::jsonb, '**My support and strengths list**

1. Write down a hard time you got through, big or small.
2. Under it, list what helped: people, habits, beliefs, places, or even luck. Be honest and generous.
3. Circle two items you could use again now.
4. Write down two people you could reach out to if things got tough, and one way to contact each.
5. Put the list somewhere you can find it.

Looking back can bring up painful memories. If it does, take a break or do it with someone you trust. This exercise builds awareness and is not a substitute for therapy or other professional care.', '[{"title":"Building your resilience","publisher":"American Psychological Association","year":"2020","url":"https://www.apa.org/topics/resilience/building-your-resilience"},{"title":"Mental health (fact sheet)","publisher":"World Health Organization","year":"2026","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-strengthening-our-response"},{"title":"Managing Stress","publisher":"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention","year":"n.d.","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/mental-health/living-with/index.html"}]'::jsonb, '["understanding-stress","understanding-loneliness","seeking-professional-help"]'::jsonb, array['mental wellness','article']::text[], false, '', 'If a stressful experience keeps you from handling daily activities, or you feel stuck, consider talking with a licensed mental health professional. Your primary care provider can also help you find one. If you are in crisis or thinking about suicide, call or text 988 (the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) or chat at chat.988lifeline.org, any time, in the U.S. For a life-threatening emergency, call 911.', 'Resilience is described in different ways across research, and what builds it depends on a person''s situation and resources. This is general education.', 4, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('seeking-professional-help', 'seeking-help', 'wellness', 'article', 'Seeking Professional Help: When and How to Reach Out', 'Reaching out for help is a strong step. This guide covers signs it may be time, who can help, and where to turn in a crisis.', 'Many people wait a long time before asking for help with their mental health. Some worry about cost. Some fear being judged. Some are not sure whether what they feel is "bad enough." This article is meant to make the first step a bit clearer. It is general information and not a replacement for care from a licensed professional.

## Signs it may be time

The National Institute of Mental Health (NIMH) says to consider professional help if severe or distressing symptoms last 2 weeks or more. Examples include trouble sleeping, changes in appetite, difficulty concentrating, loss of interest in things you enjoy, and feeling irritable or restless. NIMH also says stress or anxiety that disrupts everyday life, leads you to avoid activities, or seems always present is a sign to seek help.

You do not have to wait until things are a crisis. If something is bothering you and you would like support, that is reason enough.

## Where to start

NIMH suggests starting with your primary care provider, who can do an initial screening and refer you to a specialist. Treatment usually includes therapy, medication, or both, and the right mix is something you decide together with a licensed provider. NIMH notes that social workers, psychologists, and psychiatrists are mental health professionals who provide treatment.

Other places to look, according to NIMH:

- Your insurance company''s provider directory.
- State or county health departments.
- Your school''s health center.
- Your employer''s Employee Assistance Program (EAP), described by NIMH as a free and confidential service.
- SAMHSA''s treatment locator at findtreatment.gov.
- Medicare''s provider finder, or VA locations and providers, if those apply to you.

The Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration (SAMHSA) National Helpline is 1-800-662-HELP (4357). SAMHSA describes it as a free, confidential, 24/7, 365-day-a-year treatment referral and information service for people and families dealing with mental and/or substance use disorders. It is available in English and Spanish.

## Tips for the first conversation

- Write down what you have been feeling, when it started, and how it affects your day.
- Bring questions. NIMH suggests asking about a provider''s experience treating your issue and whether they accept your insurance.
- Bring a trusted person if that helps.
- Remember that fit matters. NIMH notes that a good relationship with your provider is important, and if treatment isn''t helping, talk with them about it.

NIMH also says: "Do not stop treatment without talking to your health care provider." If you are taking medication or are in therapy and have concerns, bring them to your provider.

## Real barriers

Cost, long waitlists, transportation, work schedules, language, and past bad experiences with health care are real barriers. Telehealth may be an option for some people. Community clinics, sliding-scale fees, and your EAP may help. If the first provider is not a fit, you can try another. That is not a failure.

## In a crisis

If you or someone you know is struggling or thinking about suicide, call or text 988 (the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline), or chat at chat.988lifeline.org. NIMH describes it as confidential and available 24 hours a day, 7 days a week. Veterans can call 988 and press 1, or text 838255. If a situation is life-threatening, call 911 or go to the nearest emergency room. After a disaster, NIMH lists the Disaster Distress Helpline at 1-800-985-5990.

**Example (fictional):** Elena has felt down and exhausted for a month. She is nervous, so she writes three notes: "Trouble sleeping, no energy, crying a lot." At her doctor''s visit, she reads them aloud. Her doctor listens, asks questions, and refers her to a counselor. The first counselor is not a good fit, so she asks for a different one. The second one is. It took effort, and it was worth it.

Asking for help is a sign of caring for yourself. You deserve support.', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["NIMH says to consider help if distressing symptoms last 2 weeks or more or disrupt daily life.","A primary care provider is a good first step and can refer you to a specialist.","Resources include findtreatment.gov and SAMHSA''s National Helpline, 1-800-662-HELP (4357).","Do not stop treatment without talking to your health care provider.","In a crisis, call or text 988. In a life-threatening emergency, call 911."]'::jsonb, '["What is making me hesitate, and what might help with that?","What would I want to tell a provider in my first visit?","Who could help me make the call or go with me?"]'::jsonb, '**Prepare for a first appointment**

1. Write down what has been bothering you, when it started, and how it affects sleep, work, and relationships.
2. Note any health conditions, medications, or past treatment (bring a list).
3. Write two or three questions, such as "What treatment options exist?" and "Do you accept my insurance?"
4. Decide on one next step: calling your primary care office, checking your insurance directory, asking about your EAP, or visiting findtreatment.gov.
5. Pick a time today or tomorrow to take that step, and tell one person you trust.

If making the call feels like too much, ask someone to sit with you while you do it. This exercise helps you prepare and does not replace professional care.', '[{"title":"Help for Mental Illnesses","publisher":"National Institute of Mental Health","year":"n.d.","url":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/find-help"},{"title":"Caring for Your Mental Health","publisher":"National Institute of Mental Health","year":"n.d.","url":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/topics/caring-for-your-mental-health"},{"title":"National Helpline","publisher":"Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration (SAMHSA)","year":"n.d.","url":"https://www.samhsa.gov/find-help/helplines/national-helpline"},{"title":"FindTreatment.gov","publisher":"Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration (SAMHSA)","year":"n.d.","url":"https://findtreatment.gov/"}]'::jsonb, '["understanding-depression","understanding-anxiety","grief-and-loss"]'::jsonb, array['mental wellness','article']::text[], true, 'This article mentions suicide and crisis resources. If you are in crisis, call or text 988 at any time in the U.S.', 'This article is about getting professional help. Start with your primary care provider, SAMHSA''s National Helpline (1-800-662-4357), or findtreatment.gov. If you are in crisis or thinking about suicide, call or text 988 (the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) or chat at chat.988lifeline.org, any time, in the U.S. For a life-threatening emergency, call 911.', 'This is general education. Services, costs, and availability vary by location, and it can take some effort to find the right provider.', 4, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('reframing', 'reframing', 'perspective', 'article', 'Reframing: Seeing the Same Situation More Than One Way', 'Reframing means looking at a situation from another reasonable angle. It can shift how you feel, but it is a tool, not a cure and not a way to excuse harm.', '## What reframing is

Reframing (researchers often call it cognitive reappraisal) means deliberately looking at a situation from a different angle so that its meaning to you changes. The facts stay the same. What changes is the story you tell about them, and often the feelings that come with that story.

Reframing is not pretending. It is not "just look on the bright side," and it is not telling yourself that something painful does not hurt. It is asking a simple question: "Is there another reasonable way to read this?" and giving that option a fair hearing next to your first reading.

## What the research says

A large meta-analysis by Webb, Miles and Sheeran (2012) pooled 306 experimental comparisons of emotion regulation strategies. Overall, changing how you think about a situation (called cognitive change) had a small-to-medium average effect on emotional outcomes (d+ = 0.36). Re-thinking the situation itself worked a bit better (0.36) than trying to re-think your own emotional reaction (0.23). Taking another person''s perspective looked especially helpful (0.45).

Those are averages across many mostly experimental studies. They do not guarantee that reframing will work for you, on this problem, today.

Another study, by Jamieson and colleagues (2010), looked at students preparing for a graduate exam. Students who were taught that the jittery, racing feeling in their body can help performance did better on the math section than a comparison group. It is a single study in a specific setting, but it shows how the meaning we give a physical sensation can matter.

## Example (fictional): The quiet meeting

Maya presents a project and her manager says almost nothing afterward. Her first interpretation is, "She hated it. I''m going to be in trouble." Her chest tightens and she spends the evening rehearsing worst cases.

Later she tries a reframe. She asks herself, "What else could a quiet reaction mean?" She writes down a few possibilities: her manager was thinking, was rushing to another meeting, or was saving feedback for a one-on-one. None of these is guaranteed. But the list makes her first story feel like one option among several, and she decides to request ten minutes for feedback instead of guessing.

## A simple way to try it

1. **Notice the first interpretation.** Write it as a sentence: "This means that ..."
2. **Ask three questions.** What else could this mean? What would I say to a friend in this spot? What might I be leaving out?
3. **Choose a version that is believable and useful.** If a new story feels fake, it will not help. A good reframe is one you can actually accept, even if it is only a little more balanced.
4. **Take one small action** that fits the new view, like asking a question or taking a break.

## When reframing is the wrong tool

Some situations are genuinely unfair, unsafe, or harmful. In those cases the right move may be to set a boundary, change the situation, or ask for help, not to reinterpret it. You are never at fault for failing to "think your way out" of circumstances you did not choose.

Reframing is also not a treatment for depression, anxiety, or other mental health conditions. Structured approaches such as cognitive behavioral therapy include learning to examine thoughts, and a large review of meta-analyses found strong support for CBT for several conditions. But doing that work with a trained clinician is different from trying reframes alone on a bad day. If your thoughts feel stuck, relentless, or frightening, that is a good reason to talk with someone qualified.
', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["Reframing means finding another reasonable reading of a situation, not denying your feelings.","Research suggests changing how you think about a situation has a small-to-medium average effect on emotions, which is helpful but not magic.","A good reframe is believable to you and leads to a small, useful action.","If a situation is unfair or unsafe, change the situation or get support instead of reinterpreting it."]'::jsonb, '["Think of a recent moment that stung. What was your first interpretation of it?","What are two other reasonable explanations you could not rule out?","What would you say to a friend who told you the same story?"]'::jsonb, '', '[{"title":"Dealing with feeling: A meta-analysis of the effectiveness of strategies derived from the process model of emotion regulation (Webb, Miles & Sheeran)","publisher":"Psychological Bulletin, American Psychological Association","year":2012,"url":"https://sussex.figshare.com/articles/journal_contribution/Dealing_with_feeling_a_meta-analysis_of_the_effectiveness_of_strategies_derived_from_the_process_model_of_emotion_regulation/23396432"},{"title":"Turning the knots in your stomach into bows: Reappraising arousal improves performance on the GRE (Jamieson, Mendes, Blackstock & Schmader)","publisher":"Journal of Experimental Social Psychology","year":2010,"url":"https://dash.harvard.edu/handle/1/4214916"},{"title":"The efficacy of cognitive behavioral therapy: A review of meta-analyses (Hofmann, Asnaani, Vonk, Sawyer & Fang)","publisher":"Cognitive Therapy and Research (via PubMed Central, NIH)","year":2012,"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3584580"}]'::jsonb, '["facts-vs-assumptions","control","decisions"]'::jsonb, array['perspective','article']::text[], false, '', 'If persistent negative thinking is affecting your sleep, work, relationships, or safety, consider talking with a licensed mental health professional. If you are in crisis in the U.S., call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline). In an emergency, call 911.', 'Research findings: meta-analytic evidence shows a small-to-medium average effect of cognitive change on emotion, and a single experiment linked reappraising arousal to exam performance. Limits: averages do not predict individual results, and most studies are short-term experiments; #ChooseHappiness uses reframing as an education tool, not a treatment.', 3, true, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('facts-vs-assumptions', 'facts-vs-assumptions', 'perspective', 'article', 'Facts vs. Assumptions: Separating What Happened From What You Added', 'Our minds fill in gaps fast and then treat the fill-in as fact. Learning to sort what you know from what you are guessing can lower the heat on a lot of everyday stress.', '## The mind loves a complete story

When something happens, your mind rarely waits for all the information. It fills gaps using past experience, current mood, and fears. That is normal and often useful. The trouble starts when a guess feels so natural that we treat it as a fact.

A text goes unanswered. A friend seems distant. A coworker''s email sounds short. Each moment can quietly turn into a conclusion: "They''re upset with me." "I''m being left out." "I''m in trouble."

## The camera test

One way to sort facts from assumptions is the camera test: would a video camera have recorded it?

- **Fact:** "I sent a message at 2 p.m. and had no reply by 6 p.m."
- **Assumption:** "She is ignoring me because she is mad."

Facts are things you can observe or verify. Interpretations, predictions, and mind reading ("he thinks I''m lazy") are assumptions, even when they feel certain. Feelings are real and worth respecting, but a strong feeling is not the same as proof of what another person intends.

## Example (fictional): The unanswered text

Dev texts a friend about weekend plans and hears nothing all evening. His mind produces a story: "She''s still annoyed about last month. I always mess things up."

He pauses and writes two columns.

*What I know:* I sent a text at 2 p.m. No reply by 8 p.m. She sometimes takes a day to respond.

*What I am adding:* She is annoyed. It is about last month. It is my fault.

Seeing them side by side, Dev notices that his "what I know" column is short and his "what I''m adding" column is long. He sends a light follow-up the next morning instead of a long apology. She replies that she had a rough day at work and her phone died. The story he told himself was louder than the evidence.

## Why checking matters

A research team led by Eyal, Steffel and Epley (2018) ran 25 experiments testing whether imagining yourself in another person''s shoes makes you better at guessing their thoughts and feelings. It did not make people more accurate. In some cases their confidence even rose while accuracy did not. When people instead learned the other person''s perspective directly, through conversation, accuracy improved. The takeaway for everyday life is simple: when something matters, asking beats guessing.

## Steps you can try

1. **Write down what happened** in camera-test language.
2. **Label your additions** as interpretations, predictions, or mind reading.
3. **List at least two other explanations**, including a boring one.
4. **Decide whether to check.** Sometimes the best step is a calm question: "Hey, I noticed X. Is everything okay?"

## Limits to keep in mind

Sometimes the assumption turns out to be right, and a hard fact is still a hard fact. This practice is not about talking yourself out of every worry. It is also not a way to dismiss your own experience. If someone repeatedly treats you badly, that pattern is information, not an assumption you need to disprove.

Examining thoughts is a core skill in cognitive behavioral therapy, which a large review of meta-analyses found to have strong support for several conditions, including anxiety disorders. If racing assumptions are taking over your days, a licensed clinician can help in ways a short article cannot.
', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["Feelings can be real while the story attached to them is still a guess.","The camera test: if a video camera could not record it, it is probably an interpretation.","Research on perspective-taking suggests that asking people directly improves accuracy more than imagining their view.","Sometimes your assumption is correct, and this practice does not require you to ignore patterns that are actually harmful."]'::jsonb, '["What is a story you told yourself recently that you never checked?","Which facts did you actually have in that moment?","Is there a kind, low-pressure question you could ask to find out more?"]'::jsonb, '', '[{"title":"Perspective mistaking: Accurately understanding the mind of another requires getting perspective, not taking perspective (Eyal, Steffel & Epley)","publisher":"Journal of Personality and Social Psychology","year":2018,"url":"https://cris.iucc.ac.il/en/publications/perspective-mistaking-accurately-understanding-the-mind-of-anothe/"},{"title":"The efficacy of cognitive behavioral therapy: A review of meta-analyses (Hofmann, Asnaani, Vonk, Sawyer & Fang)","publisher":"Cognitive Therapy and Research (via PubMed Central, NIH)","year":2012,"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3584580"}]'::jsonb, '["reframing","relationships-perspective","decisions"]'::jsonb, array['perspective','article']::text[], false, '', 'If worries and assumptions feel constant, are hard to set aside, or are affecting your daily life, consider speaking with a licensed mental health professional. If you are in crisis in the U.S., call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline). In an emergency, call 911.', 'Research findings: a 25-experiment series found that perspective taking did not improve accuracy about others'' minds, while direct information did; CBT has a broad evidence base according to a review of meta-analyses. Limits: the fact-versus-assumption worksheet is a simplified educational tool, not a clinical technique, and the research cited does not test it directly.', 3, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('control', 'control', 'perspective', 'article', 'What You Can Control, What You Can Influence, and What You Can''t', 'Stress often grows when we pour energy into things we cannot change. Sorting concerns into control, influence, and no control can point effort where it counts, without blaming anyone for circumstances they did not choose.', '## Three buckets, not two

The idea of "control" is often presented as a split between things you can change and things you cannot. Real life is messier. A more useful sort has three buckets:

- **In my control:** my actions, my effort, how I speak to others, what I choose to do next.
- **Within my influence:** things I can affect but not decide, like how a conversation goes, whether a boss says yes, or how a team responds.
- **Outside my control:** other people''s choices, the past, the weather, the economy, many health events.

Most of what keeps us up at night sits in the second and third buckets. Sorting it can make a swirling worry feel more manageable.

## Why this can matter

A large meta-analysis by Cheng, Lau and Chan (2014) reviewed 329 studies with nearly 59,000 participants on coping flexibility, which is the ability to fit your coping strategy to the situation. It found a small-to-moderate positive link between coping flexibility and psychological adjustment (r = .23). Studies that measured a person''s sense of matching strategy to situation tended to find stronger links than studies that only counted how many strategies someone had. This kind of research cannot prove cause and effect, but it fits a practical idea: the same tool does not work everywhere. Problem-solving fits problems you can act on. Rest, support, and acceptance fit things you cannot change.

## Example (fictional): The job search

Priya has been applying for jobs for two months and feels sick with worry.

*In my control:* updating her resume, sending five applications a week, asking a former coworker for feedback.

*Within my influence:* whether an interviewer remembers her, how well she prepares for each conversation.

*Outside my control:* how many people apply, a company freezing hiring, a hiring manager''s mood.

Priya cannot make an employer say yes. She can choose what she does on Tuesday morning. Naming the buckets does not guarantee a job, but it shifts her from "everything depends on me" to "some things depend on me, and I can do those well."

## Your body''s stress response is also open to interpretation

In an experiment by Jamieson and colleagues (2010), students preparing for a graduate exam were told that the physical arousal of nerves can help performance. Those students scored higher on the math section than a comparison group. It is one study, and it does not mean nerves are always helpful. It does suggest that how we interpret our body''s signals is one place we sometimes have some influence.

## Try this

1. **List what is weighing on you.** Do not edit.
2. **Sort each item** into control, influence, or no control.
3. **For each "control" item, pick one next step** small enough to do today.
4. **For each "no control" item, ask what support or comfort you need** and give yourself permission to feel what you feel about it.

## Important limits

Focusing on what you can control is not a way of saying everything is up to you. Illness, discrimination, poverty, unsafe homes, and loss are not failures of mindset, and no one chooses them. Sometimes the most powerful thing within your influence is reaching out for help, and that is a legitimate move, not a sign of weakness.

If a sense of powerlessness is constant or overwhelming, a licensed professional can help you sort through it with you.
', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["Three buckets (control, influence, no control) are more realistic than a two-way split.","Matching your coping approach to the situation is linked to better adjustment in a large meta-analysis, though that link is not proof of cause.","How you interpret body sensations of stress may be one place you have some influence.","Circumstances outside someone''s control are not their fault, and asking for help is a valid use of the control you do have."]'::jsonb, '["What is taking up the most mental space right now, and which bucket does it belong in?","What is one small thing in your control that you could do in the next 24 hours?","What would it look like to give yourself some comfort about something you cannot change?"]'::jsonb, '', '[{"title":"Coping flexibility and psychological adjustment to stressful life changes: A meta-analytic review (Cheng, Lau & Chan)","publisher":"Psychological Bulletin, American Psychological Association","year":2014,"url":"https://repository.hku.hk/handle/10722/204912"},{"title":"Turning the knots in your stomach into bows: Reappraising arousal improves performance on the GRE (Jamieson, Mendes, Blackstock & Schmader)","publisher":"Journal of Experimental Social Psychology","year":2010,"url":"https://dash.harvard.edu/handle/1/4214916"}]'::jsonb, '["reframing","setbacks","decisions"]'::jsonb, array['perspective','article']::text[], false, '', 'If you feel helpless much of the time or your circumstances feel unmanageable, a licensed mental health professional or a trusted community resource can help. If you are in crisis in the U.S., call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline). In an emergency, call 911.', 'Research findings: a meta-analysis found a small-to-moderate positive association between coping flexibility and adjustment, and one experiment linked reappraising arousal to exam performance. Limits: the associations are correlational, the exam study is a single experiment, and the three-bucket sort is a common practical framework rather than a tested intervention.', 3, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('meaning', 'meaning', 'perspective', 'article', 'Finding Meaning Without Forcing It', 'Meaning-making is the slow, personal process of fitting hard experiences into your sense of what matters. It cannot be rushed, and it is never required.', '## What meaning-making is, and is not

When something hard happens, we often ask why, or what it says about us, or where life goes from here. Psychologists call the process of working through those questions meaning-making.

It is **not** the idea that everything happens for a reason. Some things do not happen for any reason, and some pain cannot and should not be made to look like a gift. Meaning-making also does not have a deadline. It can take months or years, and it is fine to have days when you cannot see any.

## A research model in plain language

Crystal Park''s (2010) review of the meaning literature describes a model with two layers. *Global meaning* is your big-picture beliefs about how the world works and what matters to you (for example, "effort pays off" or "people can be trusted"). *Situational meaning* is how you interpret a specific event. Distress can grow when the two do not fit, such as when a layoff clashes with "if I work hard I''ll be safe." Meaning-making is the process of reducing that mismatch, either by reinterpreting the event or by adjusting the broader belief.

Park noted that theory in this area has moved faster than the research, so some parts of the model are well supported and others are not. She also suggested that the quality of meaning-making may matter as much as the amount. Going over and over the same painful question without movement is different from slowly reaching an understanding you can live with.

A longer study of 1,687 midlife adults who had experienced a very difficult event followed them for about 19 years. The researchers found that positive reappraisal was related to people''s positive and negative emotions, partly through three dimensions of meaning in life: significance, coherence, and purpose. This is observational, so it cannot show that one thing caused the other.

## Example (fictional): After the layoff

Tom spent 22 years at one company before a restructuring ended his job. For a while he repeats the same questions: "Why me? What was it all for?"

Over several months, talking with his sister and writing in a notebook, he starts to notice something. What he valued most was not the title, it was mentoring younger coworkers. That does not make the layoff "okay." He is still angry and still worried about money. But the thread of what matters to him gives him a direction as he looks for his next role.

## Gentle ways to explore meaning

- **Ask about values rather than reasons.** "What matters to me, and how did this touch that?" is easier to answer than "Why did this happen?"
- **Look for small, true things.** Not silver linings you do not believe, but honest observations: who showed up, what you learned about yourself, what you now refuse to take for granted.
- **Talk or write.** Putting the story into words, with someone safe or on paper, can help organize it.
- **Let it be unfinished.** You can hold both "this was terrible" and "I am still figuring out what it means."

## Limits

Searching for meaning should never be used to tell someone to be grateful for harm. If you are in a harmful situation now, safety and support come before meaning. If the weight of a loss or a painful event is not easing, or keeps intruding on your days, a licensed therapist can offer structured support at your pace.
', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["Meaning-making is a slow, personal process, not a requirement and not the claim that everything happens for a reason.","Distress can grow when a hard event clashes with your deeper beliefs, and meaning-making works to close that gap.","Asking what matters to you can be gentler and more useful than asking why it happened.","It is fine for meaning to stay unfinished."]'::jsonb, '["What do you care about most, and how did a recent hard time touch that?","Is there a question you keep circling that might be better asked a different way?","Who could you talk to, or what could you write, to help you work through this at your own pace?"]'::jsonb, '', '[{"title":"Making sense of the meaning literature: An integrative review of meaning making and its effects on adjustment to stressful life events (Park)","publisher":"Psychological Bulletin, American Psychological Association","year":2010,"url":"https://ovc.ojp.gov/sites/g/files/xyckuh226/files/media/document/os_meaning_making_review.pdf"},{"title":"A longitudinal investigation of the meaning-making model in midlife adults who have experienced trauma (Fitzke, Marsh & Prince)","publisher":"Journal of Clinical Psychology","year":2021,"url":"https://midus.wisc.edu/findings/pdfs/2416.pdf"}]'::jsonb, '["gratitude","setbacks","reframing"]'::jsonb, array['perspective','article']::text[], true, 'This article briefly mentions research on people who have experienced traumatic or very difficult events.', 'If an event or loss continues to cause strong distress, intrusive memories, or trouble functioning, please consider speaking with a licensed mental health professional, ideally one experienced in trauma or grief. If you are in crisis in the U.S., call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline). In an emergency, call 911.', 'Research findings: Park''s review proposes a meaning-making model and notes that theory has outpaced empirical testing; a long-term observational study found that positive reappraisal was related to emotions partly through meaning in life. Limits: the studies are largely correlational, and meaning-making is a research concept, not a guaranteed path to feeling better.', 3, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('gratitude', 'gratitude', 'perspective', 'article', 'Gratitude That Isn''t Forced', 'Gratitude practices have modest support in research, and they work best when they are honest and specific. They are not a treatment, and they are not a duty.', '## A small tool with modest evidence

Gratitude practices ask you to notice and appreciate good things in your life, often by writing them down or telling someone thank you. They are popular, and sometimes oversold. The honest picture from research is that they can help a little for some people, not that they transform lives.

A meta-analysis by Cregg and Cheavens (2021) combined 27 studies with 3,675 participants and looked at symptoms of depression and anxiety. Gratitude interventions had a small effect on those symptoms right after the intervention (g = -0.29). The authors also noted that earlier reviews had raised the possibility that some benefits could reflect placebo effects.

A broader systematic review published in 2023 (Diniz and colleagues) included 64 randomized trials, 33 of them in the meta-analysis. It found that gratitude interventions were associated with greater gratitude, higher life satisfaction, and fewer anxiety and depression symptoms. The reviewers also cautioned that most studies had a high risk of bias, certainty in the evidence was mostly low or very low, and measures varied widely. In other words: promising, modest, and not yet settled.

## What "not forced" means

Gratitude does not mean:

- feeling thankful for something painful,
- hiding anger, grief, or fear under a smile,
- comparing yourself with people who have it worse ("at least..."), or
- owing anyone a good mood.

You can feel appreciation for one thing and sadness or frustration about another in the same hour. Both are true, and neither cancels the other.

## Example (fictional): The tired caregiver

Jordan cares for a parent with dementia and is exhausted. Someone suggests a gratitude journal, and Jordan nearly rolls their eyes. "Be thankful for what? This is hard."

Instead of a list of big blessings, Jordan tries something smaller and honest. Three nights a week, Jordan writes one specific thing that helped that day: "The neighbor dropped off soup." "Dad laughed at the dog." "The pharmacy line was short." Jordan also keeps a second line for "what was hard today," because leaving it out feels fake. Some nights the gratitude line stays blank, and that is allowed. After a few weeks, Jordan notices the small helpful moments more easily, but the exhaustion and grief are still there, and Jordan keeps seeking caregiver support.

## Ways to practice

1. **Be specific.** "My friend texted to check on me after my appointment" lands better than "I''m grateful for friends."
2. **Focus on people and details**, not just possessions.
3. **Start small and keep it short.** Two or three sentences, a few times a week, is plenty.
4. **Say it to the person**, if you want. A brief thank-you message can be meaningful.
5. **Skip it when it feels hollow.** If a practice feels like another chore, put it down.

## Limits

Gratitude practices are not a treatment for depression or anxiety, and they do not replace therapy, medication, or other care. They should never be used to tell someone to be grateful for a harmful situation. If low mood, worry, or numbness lasts for weeks or interferes with daily life, please consider speaking with a licensed professional.
', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["Research suggests gratitude practices can have a small benefit for some people, but evidence quality is mixed.","Specific, honest gratitude tends to feel more real than general lists.","You can feel grateful and sad, angry, or worried at the same time.","Gratitude is optional, and it is not a substitute for professional care."]'::jsonb, '["What is one specific, small thing from today that helped, even a little?","Is there a person you would want to thank, even briefly?","Where might gratitude be feeling forced for you, and what could you do instead?"]'::jsonb, '', '[{"title":"Gratitude interventions: Effective self-help? A meta-analysis of the impact on symptoms of depression and anxiety (Cregg & Cheavens)","publisher":"Journal of Happiness Studies","year":2021,"url":"https://ideas.repec.org/a/spr/jhappi/v22y2021i1d10.1007_s10902-020-00236-6.html"},{"title":"The effects of gratitude interventions: a systematic review and meta-analysis (Diniz et al.)","publisher":"Einstein (Sao Paulo), via PubMed Central, NIH","year":2023,"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10393216"}]'::jsonb, '["meaning","setbacks","reframing"]'::jsonb, array['perspective','article']::text[], false, '', 'If sadness, anxiety, or emptiness lasts for weeks or interferes with daily life, please talk with a licensed mental health professional or your doctor. If you are in crisis in the U.S., call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline). In an emergency, call 911.', 'Research findings: two meta-analyses report small average improvements in anxiety and depression symptoms, and the 2023 review rates most evidence as low certainty with a high risk of bias. Limits: gratitude practices are a supportive habit, not a treatment, and results vary from person to person.', 3, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('setbacks', 'setbacks', 'perspective', 'article', 'Setbacks: Treating a Stumble as Information', 'After a failure or disappointment, being hard on yourself can feel like motivation. Research suggests a kinder approach may keep people trying without the extra sting.', '## The harsh inner coach

Many of us believe that if we stop being tough on ourselves after a mistake, we will stop trying. The inner critic feels like discipline: "Don''t let yourself off the hook."

But harshness and effort are not the same thing. Self-criticism often adds shame on top of the disappointment, and shame tends to make people want to hide, not to try again.

## What studies suggest

Breines and Chen (2012) ran four experiments to test whether self-compassion, meaning treating yourself with the kindness you would offer a friend, would sap motivation to improve. Compared with comparison groups, people who practiced self-compassion held a more flexible view of a personal weakness, felt more motivated to make amends after a moral mistake, and, in one experiment, spent more time studying for a difficult test after failing an earlier one. Their conclusion was that accepting your failures may, somewhat paradoxically, make you more motivated to improve. Four experiments are a starting point, not a verdict.

A separate meta-analysis by Zessin and colleagues (2015) pooled 79 samples with over 16,000 participants and found that higher self-compassion went with better well-being overall (r = .47). Most of that evidence is correlational, so we cannot say self-compassion causes well-being, although a smaller group of studies in the review suggested it might.

## What self-compassion is not

It is not making excuses, ignoring consequences, or lowering your standards. It means acknowledging that the thing hurt, remembering that struggling is part of being human, and speaking to yourself in a way that helps you get back up.

## Example (fictional): The failed exam

Alicia studied for months for a professional certification exam and did not pass. Her first thought is, "I''m a fraud. Everyone else passes."

She pauses and tries three steps.

1. **Name what happened without a verdict:** "I did not pass this attempt. That stings."
2. **Treat it like information:** What went well? Which sections were weakest? Was the study plan the problem, or the test conditions?
3. **Pick a next step:** She emails the testing body about the retake schedule and asks a colleague who passed how they studied.

She still feels disappointed. But she moves from "I am a failure" to "I have a result and a plan," which is easier to work with.

## When you are in the middle of one

- **Say what is true and hard.** "This is a real loss," not "It''s fine."
- **Ask the friend question.** "What would I say to someone I care about in this exact spot?"
- **Separate the event from your worth.** A result is one data point, not your identity.
- **Choose the smallest next step**, then rest.
- **Let others in.** Disappointment is easier to carry with company.

## Limits

Not every setback is small. Major losses, such as the end of a relationship, a health crisis, or job loss, can bring grief that needs time and support, not a quick bounce back. There is no schedule you are failing. And some setbacks happen because of systems, circumstances, or other people, not because of anything you did, and you are not to blame for those.

If a setback leaves you feeling hopeless, worthless, or unable to function for more than a couple of weeks, please reach out to a licensed professional.
', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["Harsh self-criticism after a failure can feel productive, but experiments suggest self-compassion may keep motivation going while easing the sting.","Self-compassion is not excuse-making, it is speaking to yourself the way you would to a friend.","Treat a setback as information, then choose one small next step.","Big losses need time and support, and not every setback is your fault."]'::jsonb, '["What do you tell yourself when something goes wrong, and would you say it to a friend?","If this setback were information, what might it be telling you?","What is the smallest next step you could take, and who could you ask for help?"]'::jsonb, '', '[{"title":"Self-compassion increases self-improvement motivation (Breines & Chen)","publisher":"Personality and Social Psychology Bulletin","year":2012,"url":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0146167212445599"},{"title":"The relationship between self-compassion and well-being: A meta-analysis (Zessin, Dickhauser & Garbade)","publisher":"Applied Psychology: Health and Well-Being","year":2015,"url":"https://self-compassion.org/wp-content/uploads/2015/09/Zessin.meta-analysis.pdf"}]'::jsonb, '["gratitude","control","meaning"]'::jsonb, array['perspective','article']::text[], false, '', 'If a setback leaves you feeling hopeless, worthless, or unable to function for more than a couple of weeks, please talk with a licensed mental health professional. If you are in crisis in the U.S., call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline). In an emergency, call 911.', 'Research findings: four experiments found self-compassion was linked to greater motivation to improve, and a meta-analysis found self-compassion goes along with well-being (a correlation). Limits: much of the evidence is correlational, the experiments are few, and self-compassion is not a substitute for care after serious loss.', 3, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('relationships-perspective', 'relationships-perspective', 'perspective', 'article', 'Perspective in Relationships: Getting It, Not Just Guessing It', 'Understanding another person takes more than imagining their view. Curiosity and conversation tend to work better than mind reading, with an important exception: you do not owe empathy to someone who harms you.', '## Two people, two stories

Most conflicts are not about one person being right. Two people lived the same moment with different information, different moods, and different histories. Each tells a story that makes sense from the inside.

Perspective-taking is the skill of stepping, even briefly, into the other person''s view. It can reduce defensiveness and open up better conversations. But it has a trap.

## Taking perspective vs. getting perspective

Eyal, Steffel and Epley (2018) ran 25 experiments on whether being told to take another person''s perspective makes you better at knowing what they think and feel. It did not improve accuracy. Accuracy sometimes dipped slightly while confidence sometimes rose. When people learned the other person''s perspective directly, through conversation, accuracy did improve. The authors summarize it as getting perspective, not simply taking it.

In everyday terms: "If I were them, I''d be mad" is a guess. "You seemed quiet at dinner. What was going on for you?" is information.

## A different benefit of stepping back

Perspective can also help with your own side of a conflict. Grossmann and Kross (2014) found, across three experiments with 693 participants, that people reasoned more wisely about someone else''s relationship conflict than their own. This included recognizing limits to their own knowledge, valuing compromise, and considering other viewpoints. When people were asked to self-distance, meaning to look at their conflict as an outside observer would, that gap went away.

Across the broader emotion-regulation literature, a meta-analysis by Webb, Miles and Sheeran (2012) found that perspective taking was one of the more effective forms of cognitive change (d+ = 0.45). It can calm things down, as long as we do not mistake it for knowing.

## Example (fictional): The dishes

Sam is irritated that Riley left the dishes again. His first story: "Riley doesn''t respect me."

He tries the observer view: if a neighbor watched this argument, what would they see? Two tired people and a sink. Then he gets perspective instead of assuming it. "Hey, the dishes have been piling up and it''s bugging me. How has your week been?" Riley explains that a deadline has swallowed their evenings and they feel guilty. They agree on a swap for two weeks. Sam did not have to decide Riley was right, only to find out what was really going on.

## Tools to try

- **Ask, then listen.** One open question beats three guesses.
- **Name your guess out loud.** "I might be wrong, but I''m wondering if..."
- **Take the observer view** before replying in a heated moment.
- **Aim for understanding, not agreement.** You can understand someone and still disagree.
- **Take a break** if either of you is too upset to hear anything.

## When this does not apply

Perspective-taking is for ordinary conflicts between people who basically respect each other. It is not a reason to keep seeing the best in someone who controls, frightens, or hurts you. If you are not safe in a relationship, understanding their point of view is not the goal. Your safety is. A licensed counselor, a trusted person, or a domestic violence service can help you plan.
', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["Research suggests imagining someone''s view does not reliably make you more accurate, while asking them does.","Looking at your own conflict from an observer''s view may help you reason more wisely about it.","Understanding someone does not mean agreeing with them or excusing harm.","In unsafe relationships, safety comes before perspective-taking."]'::jsonb, '["Where in a current relationship are you guessing what the other person thinks?","What is one open, curious question you could ask this week?","If a neutral observer watched your last disagreement, what might they notice?"]'::jsonb, '', '[{"title":"Perspective mistaking: Accurately understanding the mind of another requires getting perspective, not taking perspective (Eyal, Steffel & Epley)","publisher":"Journal of Personality and Social Psychology","year":2018,"url":"https://cris.iucc.ac.il/en/publications/perspective-mistaking-accurately-understanding-the-mind-of-anothe/"},{"title":"Exploring Solomon''s paradox: Self-distancing eliminates the self-other asymmetry in wise reasoning about close relationships in younger and older adults (Grossmann & Kross)","publisher":"Psychological Science","year":2014,"url":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0956797614535400"},{"title":"Dealing with feeling: A meta-analysis of the effectiveness of strategies derived from the process model of emotion regulation (Webb, Miles & Sheeran)","publisher":"Psychological Bulletin, American Psychological Association","year":2012,"url":"https://sussex.figshare.com/articles/journal_contribution/Dealing_with_feeling_a_meta-analysis_of_the_effectiveness_of_strategies_derived_from_the_process_model_of_emotion_regulation/23396432"}]'::jsonb, '["facts-vs-assumptions","decisions","reframing"]'::jsonb, array['perspective','article']::text[], true, 'This article briefly mentions unsafe or harmful relationships.', 'If conflicts at home or work are wearing you down, or if you feel unsafe or controlled in a relationship, please reach out to a licensed counselor, a trusted person, or a domestic violence service. If you are in crisis in the U.S., call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline). In an emergency, call 911.', 'Research findings: a 25-experiment series found that perspective taking did not improve accuracy about others'' minds while direct information did; three experiments found self-distancing improved wise reasoning about conflicts; a meta-analysis found perspective taking was among the more effective forms of cognitive change. Limits: these were mostly controlled experiments, and they do not address relationships involving harm.', 3, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('decisions', 'decisions', 'perspective', 'article', 'Decisions: Stepping Back to See More Clearly', 'We often reason better about a friend''s problem than our own. Creating a little distance from a decision can help you weigh it more wisely, though some choices also need facts, time, and support.', '## Why our own decisions feel harder

When a decision is close to home, emotion and urgency crowd the view. Worries about what others will think, fear of regret, and tiredness can narrow our attention to a few vivid details. The same problem is often clearer when it belongs to someone else.

This is sometimes called Solomon''s paradox: we can give wise advice to others while struggling with our own situation.

## What research suggests

Grossmann and Kross (2014) ran three experiments with 693 participants. People reasoned more wisely about someone else''s relationship conflict than about their own, for example by recognizing the limits of their knowledge, valuing compromise, and taking other viewpoints into account. When people were asked to self-distance (to view their own situation from an outside perspective) rather than immerse themselves in it, the gap between self and other disappeared. The study included younger and older adults, and age did not predict wiser reasoning about personal conflicts.

These findings are from controlled experiments about interpersonal conflict. They suggest a useful habit for decisions, though they do not prove that distance fixes every choice.

Webb, Miles and Sheeran''s (2012) meta-analysis of emotion regulation found that perspective taking was one of the more effective forms of cognitive change (d+ = 0.45), which fits with the idea that changing your vantage point can shift how a situation feels.

## Example (fictional): The job offer

Chris has an offer for a role with a higher salary but a longer commute. After a week of going back and forth, Chris is exhausted and leaning toward saying yes mostly to end the stress.

Chris tries three distance questions on paper:

1. **What would I tell a close friend in this exact situation?** "I''d ask what a longer commute does to your week, and what the pay gap means for your goals."
2. **How will this look in a year? In five?** The pay raise matters, but so does the lost time with family.
3. **What am I missing?** Chris realizes they have not asked about remote days. A short email to the employer turns up an option that changes the picture.

Chris still feels torn, but now has better information and a clearer sense of what matters.

## A short decision routine

- **Name the decision in one sentence** and the real deadline, if there is one.
- **Write what you know and what you are assuming.**
- **Step back:** What would I advise a friend? What would I notice if I were watching from outside?
- **Check your state.** Are you hungry, exhausted, or flooded with emotion? If the choice can wait, wait.
- **Ask someone you trust** to poke holes in your thinking.
- **Take a small, reversible step** when you can, before a big one.

## Limits

Perspective is only one input. Good decisions also need accurate information, time, and sometimes professional advice on money, health, or legal matters. If you are in a crisis or going through severe depression or anxiety, thoughts can feel more certain than they are, so avoid making major irreversible decisions alone and lean on someone you trust or a professional. You are never required to decide faster than you are able to.
', '', '', '', '[]'::jsonb, '["We often reason more wisely about other people''s problems than our own.","In experiments, viewing a conflict from an outside perspective closed that gap.","Ask what you would tell a friend, how this looks in a year, and what you may be missing.","Big decisions made under severe stress or depression deserve extra time and support."]'::jsonb, '["What decision are you weighing, and what would you tell a friend in the same spot?","How might this look to you a year from now?","What information or person could help you see what you are missing?"]'::jsonb, '', '[{"title":"Exploring Solomon''s paradox: Self-distancing eliminates the self-other asymmetry in wise reasoning about close relationships in younger and older adults (Grossmann & Kross)","publisher":"Psychological Science","year":2014,"url":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0956797614535400"},{"title":"Dealing with feeling: A meta-analysis of the effectiveness of strategies derived from the process model of emotion regulation (Webb, Miles & Sheeran)","publisher":"Psychological Bulletin, American Psychological Association","year":2012,"url":"https://sussex.figshare.com/articles/journal_contribution/Dealing_with_feeling_a_meta-analysis_of_the_effectiveness_of_strategies_derived_from_the_process_model_of_emotion_regulation/23396432"}]'::jsonb, '["facts-vs-assumptions","control","relationships-perspective"]'::jsonb, array['perspective','article']::text[], false, '', 'If a decision feels impossible because of persistent anxiety, low mood, or crisis, please consider speaking with a licensed mental health professional, and for money, legal, or medical choices, a qualified advisor. If you are in crisis in the U.S., call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline). In an emergency, call 911.', 'Research findings: three experiments found self-distancing helped people reason about their own conflicts as wisely as about others'', and a meta-analysis found perspective taking was among the more effective forms of cognitive change. Limits: the experiments focused on relationship conflicts rather than all decisions, and distancing does not replace information or advice.', 3, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('reframe-a-situation', 'reframing', 'perspective', 'exercise', 'Try a Reframe', 'Take one stressful moment and look at it from a few reasonable angles, then choose a view you can actually believe.', '', 'Reframing means looking at a situation from another reasonable angle. It is not about pretending things are fine. The goal is to see whether there is a more balanced reading that you can believe.

Pick something small to medium in size for your first try, not the hardest thing in your life. Your answers are private and you can save them if you like.', '**Example (fictional):** Maya''s manager said almost nothing after her presentation.

- *Situation:* Quiet reaction after my presentation.
- *First thought:* She hated it and I''m in trouble.
- *Other readings:* She was rushed. She was thinking. She prefers to give feedback one on one.
- *Balanced view I can believe:* I don''t know yet, and I can ask for feedback.
- *Small step:* Request ten minutes on Thursday.', 'It is completely okay if this did not help today. Some days a reflection just does not land, and that says nothing about you. You can come back to it later, or skip it.', '[{"id":"s1","prompt":"What happened? Describe the situation in a sentence or two, like a camera would record it.","help":"Stick to what could be seen or heard.","type":"text"},{"id":"s2","prompt":"What was your first interpretation or thought about it?","help":"Write it just as it showed up, even if it feels harsh.","type":"text"},{"id":"s3","prompt":"How strongly do you believe that first thought right now?","help":"There is no right answer, this just gives you a starting point.","type":"scale","min":0,"max":10,"label_min":"Not at all","label_max":"Completely"},{"id":"s4","prompt":"What are two or three other reasonable ways to read the situation?","help":"Include at least one boring or neutral explanation.","type":"text"},{"id":"s5","prompt":"What is a more balanced view that you could actually believe?","help":"If it feels fake, make it smaller and more honest.","type":"text"},{"id":"s6","prompt":"What is one small step that fits this view?","help":"For example, asking a question, taking a break, or doing nothing yet.","type":"text"}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '', '[{"title":"Dealing with feeling: A meta-analysis of the effectiveness of strategies derived from the process model of emotion regulation (Webb, Miles & Sheeran)","publisher":"Psychological Bulletin, American Psychological Association","year":2012,"url":"https://sussex.figshare.com/articles/journal_contribution/Dealing_with_feeling_a_meta-analysis_of_the_effectiveness_of_strategies_derived_from_the_process_model_of_emotion_regulation/23396432"}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, array['perspective','guided exercise']::text[], false, '', 'If distressing thoughts are persistent or affecting your daily life, consider talking with a licensed mental health professional. If you are in crisis in the U.S., call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline). In an emergency, call 911.', '', 8, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('facts-vs-assumptions-sort', 'facts-vs-assumptions', 'perspective', 'exercise', 'Sort Facts From Assumptions', 'Split a worry into what you know and what you are adding, then decide whether a gentle check-in would help.', '', 'When we are stressed, guesses can feel like facts. This exercise helps you pull the two apart. A good test is the camera test: would a video camera have recorded it?

Some of your assumptions may turn out to be right. The point is to know which is which.', '**Example (fictional):** Dev''s friend has not answered his text.

- *Facts:* I texted at 2 p.m. No reply by 8 p.m. She sometimes takes a day to reply.
- *Assumptions:* She''s annoyed. It''s about last month. It''s my fault.
- *Other explanations:* Busy day. Phone died. Forgot to answer.
- *Next step:* Send a light message tomorrow morning.', 'It is completely okay if this did not help today. Some days a reflection just does not land, and that says nothing about you. You can come back to it later, or skip it.', '[{"id":"s1","prompt":"What is the situation that is on your mind?","help":"One or two sentences is plenty.","type":"text"},{"id":"s2","prompt":"List the facts: what could a camera have recorded?","help":"Times, words, actions. Leave out motives and predictions.","type":"text"},{"id":"s3","prompt":"List what you are adding: interpretations, predictions, or mind reading.","help":"Phrases like ''they probably'', ''I bet'', or ''always'' are clues.","type":"text"},{"id":"s4","prompt":"How certain do you feel about your biggest assumption?","help":"Rate it honestly, even if it feels very certain.","type":"scale","min":0,"max":10,"label_min":"Just a guess","label_max":"Completely certain"},{"id":"s5","prompt":"What are two other possible explanations?","help":"Include one that is ordinary or boring.","type":"text"},{"id":"s6","prompt":"What would help most right now?","help":"Pick the option that feels most doable.","type":"choice","options":["Ask the person a calm, open question","Wait a bit and see what happens","Gather more information first","Talk it through with someone I trust","Let it go for now"]}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '', '[{"title":"The efficacy of cognitive behavioral therapy: A review of meta-analyses (Hofmann, Asnaani, Vonk, Sawyer & Fang)","publisher":"Cognitive Therapy and Research (via PubMed Central, NIH)","year":2012,"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3584580"},{"title":"Perspective mistaking: Accurately understanding the mind of another requires getting perspective, not taking perspective (Eyal, Steffel & Epley)","publisher":"Journal of Personality and Social Psychology","year":2018,"url":"https://cris.iucc.ac.il/en/publications/perspective-mistaking-accurately-understanding-the-mind-of-anothe/"}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, array['perspective','guided exercise']::text[], false, '', 'If worries or assumptions feel constant or hard to set aside, consider talking with a licensed mental health professional. If you are in crisis in the U.S., call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline). In an emergency, call 911.', '', 7, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('control-map', 'control', 'perspective', 'exercise', 'Map What You Can Control', 'Sort a current worry into what is in your control, within your influence, and outside your control, then choose one small step.', '', 'When a worry feels huge, it often helps to separate the parts. Some are yours to act on, some you can only influence, and some are out of anyone''s hands. None of this means everything is your fault. Many things people face are not their choice.

Go at your own pace and skip any step you want to.', '**Example (fictional):** Priya''s job search.

- *In my control:* Updating my resume. Sending five applications a week.
- *Within my influence:* How I prepare for interviews. Who remembers me.
- *Outside my control:* Hiring freezes. How many others apply.
- *One step for today:* Email a former coworker for feedback.
- *Comfort for the rest:* Call a friend after a tough day and take a walk.', 'It is completely okay if this did not help today. Some days a reflection just does not land, and that says nothing about you. You can come back to it later, or skip it.', '[{"id":"s1","prompt":"What is the concern that is weighing on you?","help":"Name it in a sentence or two.","type":"text"},{"id":"s2","prompt":"What parts are fully in your control (your actions, effort, words, next choices)?","help":"Think about what you could do this week.","type":"text"},{"id":"s3","prompt":"What parts are within your influence, but not your decision?","help":"For example, how a conversation goes or how others respond.","type":"text"},{"id":"s4","prompt":"What parts are outside your control?","help":"The past, other people''s choices, and many outside events belong here.","type":"text"},{"id":"s5","prompt":"What is one small step you can take on something in your control?","help":"Make it small enough to do in a day.","type":"text"},{"id":"s6","prompt":"For the parts you cannot control, what support, rest, or comfort would help?","help":"This could be a person, a break, or just permission to feel it.","type":"text"}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '', '[{"title":"Coping flexibility and psychological adjustment to stressful life changes: A meta-analytic review (Cheng, Lau & Chan)","publisher":"Psychological Bulletin, American Psychological Association","year":2014,"url":"https://repository.hku.hk/handle/10722/204912"}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, array['perspective','guided exercise']::text[], false, '', 'If you feel powerless or overwhelmed much of the time, consider talking with a licensed mental health professional or a trusted community resource. If you are in crisis in the U.S., call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline). In an emergency, call 911.', '', 10, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('gratitude-specifics', 'gratitude', 'perspective', 'exercise', 'Specific, Honest Gratitude', 'Notice a few small, specific things that helped, without pushing away what is hard.', '', 'This is a gentle gratitude practice that does not ask you to feel thankful for anything painful. It asks you to notice small, specific things that helped, and also gives space to what was hard.

If nothing comes to mind, that is okay. You can write ''nothing today'' and move on.', '**Example (fictional):** Jordan, a tired caregiver.

- *Something small that helped:* The neighbor dropped off soup.
- *Why it mattered:* I did not have to cook, and it felt like someone saw me.
- *Something that was hard:* Dad did not recognize me at lunch.
- *Anyone to thank:* I will text the neighbor tonight.', 'It is completely okay if this did not help today. Some days a reflection just does not land, and that says nothing about you. You can come back to it later, or skip it.', '[{"id":"s1","prompt":"What is one small, specific thing from today (or this week) that helped, even a little?","help":"Details beat generalities. ''My friend texted after my appointment'' is better than ''friends''.","type":"text"},{"id":"s2","prompt":"Why did it matter to you?","help":"A sentence is enough.","type":"text"},{"id":"s3","prompt":"Is there something that was hard today that you want to name too?","help":"Gratitude and difficulty can sit side by side.","type":"text"},{"id":"s4","prompt":"Is there someone you might want to thank?","help":"No pressure. Pick what feels right.","type":"choice","options":["Yes, I will send a short message","Maybe later","Not this time"]},{"id":"s5","prompt":"How did this feel to do?","help":"Be honest. It is fine if it felt forced.","type":"scale","min":0,"max":10,"label_min":"Forced or flat","label_max":"Warm and real"}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '', '[{"title":"Gratitude interventions: Effective self-help? A meta-analysis of the impact on symptoms of depression and anxiety (Cregg & Cheavens)","publisher":"Journal of Happiness Studies","year":2021,"url":"https://ideas.repec.org/a/spr/jhappi/v22y2021i1d10.1007_s10902-020-00236-6.html"}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, array['perspective','guided exercise']::text[], false, '', 'If sadness, anxiety, or numbness lasts for weeks or interferes with daily life, please talk with a licensed mental health professional or your doctor. If you are in crisis in the U.S., call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline). In an emergency, call 911.', '', 6, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('setback-reset', 'setbacks', 'perspective', 'exercise', 'After a Setback: A Kind Reset', 'Name what happened, speak to yourself the way you would to a friend, and choose a small next step.', '', 'Setbacks hurt. This exercise helps you describe what happened without a harsh verdict, respond to yourself with some kindness, and find a small next step.

If the setback is a major loss, go gently and consider sharing it with someone you trust.', '**Example (fictional):** Alicia did not pass a certification exam.

- *What happened:* I did not pass this attempt.
- *What I am telling myself:* I''m a fraud.
- *What I would tell a friend:* One result is not your worth. You can retake it, and you worked hard.
- *What I can learn:* My weakest sections were X and Y.
- *Small next step:* Email about the retake schedule.', 'It is completely okay if this did not help today. Some days a reflection just does not land, and that says nothing about you. You can come back to it later, or skip it.', '[{"id":"s1","prompt":"What happened? Describe it without a verdict on yourself.","help":"''I did not get the job'' rather than ''I''m a failure''.","type":"text"},{"id":"s2","prompt":"What are you telling yourself about it?","help":"Write the harsh voice honestly. It is easier to deal with once it is on paper.","type":"text"},{"id":"s3","prompt":"What would you say to a friend who had this exact setback?","help":"Write it in the kind, honest tone you would use with them.","type":"text"},{"id":"s4","prompt":"What might this experience be telling you (information, not a verdict)?","help":"Some of it may be about circumstances you did not control.","type":"text"},{"id":"s5","prompt":"What is one small next step, or one person you could lean on?","help":"It can also be rest, if that is what you need.","type":"text"},{"id":"s6","prompt":"How are you feeling now compared with when you started?","help":"There is no right answer.","type":"scale","min":0,"max":10,"label_min":"Heavier","label_max":"A bit lighter"}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '', '[{"title":"Self-compassion increases self-improvement motivation (Breines & Chen)","publisher":"Personality and Social Psychology Bulletin","year":2012,"url":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0146167212445599"},{"title":"The relationship between self-compassion and well-being: A meta-analysis (Zessin, Dickhauser & Garbade)","publisher":"Applied Psychology: Health and Well-Being","year":2015,"url":"https://self-compassion.org/wp-content/uploads/2015/09/Zessin.meta-analysis.pdf"}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, array['perspective','guided exercise']::text[], false, '', 'If a setback leaves you feeling hopeless or worthless, or you cannot function for more than a couple of weeks, please talk with a licensed mental health professional. If you are in crisis in the U.S., call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline). In an emergency, call 911.', '', 8, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('decision-step-back', 'decisions', 'perspective', 'exercise', 'Step Back From a Decision', 'Look at a decision from the outside by asking what you would tell a friend and how it may look in a year.', '', 'It is often easier to see a friend''s situation clearly than our own. This exercise helps you borrow that outside view for a decision you are weighing.

If you are exhausted or very upset, it may help to do this later. Big choices do not have to be made in a hurry.', '**Example (fictional):** Chris has a job offer with higher pay and a longer commute.

- *Decision:* Take the offer or stay.
- *Advice to a friend:* Ask what the commute does to your week and what the pay means for your goals.
- *In a year:* The money helps, but lost family time may matter more.
- *What I may be missing:* Whether remote days are possible.
- *Next step:* Email the employer to ask.', 'It is completely okay if this did not help today. Some days a reflection just does not land, and that says nothing about you. You can come back to it later, or skip it.', '[{"id":"s1","prompt":"What decision are you weighing? State it in one sentence.","help":"Include a real deadline if there is one.","type":"text"},{"id":"s2","prompt":"If a close friend asked you for advice about this exact situation, what would you tell them?","help":"Write it as if you were talking to them.","type":"text"},{"id":"s3","prompt":"How might this look in a year? In five years?","help":"Imagine looking back from the future.","type":"text"},{"id":"s4","prompt":"What might you be missing? What information or whose view would help?","help":"Include facts you could look up and people you could ask.","type":"text"},{"id":"s5","prompt":"How are you feeling right now (rested and calm, or tired and flooded)?","help":"Strong stress can make choices feel more urgent than they are.","type":"choice","options":["Calm enough to decide","Somewhat stressed","Very stressed or exhausted","Not sure"]},{"id":"s6","prompt":"What is one small, reversible step you could take next?","help":"For example, asking a question or sleeping on it.","type":"text"}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '', '[{"title":"Exploring Solomon''s paradox: Self-distancing eliminates the self-other asymmetry in wise reasoning about close relationships in younger and older adults (Grossmann & Kross)","publisher":"Psychological Science","year":2014,"url":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0956797614535400"}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, array['perspective','guided exercise']::text[], false, '', 'If a decision feels impossible because of persistent anxiety or low mood, consider talking with a licensed mental health professional, and for money, legal, or medical choices, a qualified advisor. If you are in crisis in the U.S., call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline). In an emergency, call 911.', '', 10, false, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.articles (slug, topic_slug, pillar, kind, title, summary, body, intro, example, closing, steps, takeaways, prompts, exercise, sources, related, tags, sensitive, content_note, professional_note, evidence_note, reading_minutes, featured, published, published_at)
values ('mindful-pause', 'mindfulness', 'wellness', 'exercise', 'A Mindful Pause', 'A short pause to notice where your attention is, what you feel in your body, and what you need.', '', 'A mindful pause is a brief check-in. You are not trying to clear your mind or feel calm. You are just noticing, with some kindness, what is here right now.

Find a comfortable position. You can keep your eyes open. Take as much or as little time as you like.', '**Example (fictional):** Sam pauses before a meeting.

- *Attention:* Racing ahead to what could go wrong.
- *Body:* Tight shoulders, shallow breathing.
- *Feelings:* Nervous.
- *What I need:* Three slow breaths and a drink of water.', 'It is completely okay if this did not help today. Some days a reflection just does not land, and that says nothing about you. You can come back to it later, or skip it.', '[{"id":"s1","prompt":"Where is your attention right now? (The past, the future, a worry, this moment?)","help":"Just notice, with no judgment.","type":"text"},{"id":"s2","prompt":"What do you notice in your body?","help":"Check your shoulders, jaw, stomach, and breath.","type":"text"},{"id":"s3","prompt":"What feelings are here?","help":"Name them in a word or two.","type":"text"},{"id":"s4","prompt":"How tense or stressed do you feel right now?","help":"There is no right answer.","type":"scale","min":0,"max":10,"label_min":"Calm","label_max":"Very tense"},{"id":"s5","prompt":"Take three slow breaths. What do you need next?","help":"A stretch, water, a walk, a conversation, or nothing at all.","type":"text"}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, '', '[{"title":"Meditation programs for psychological stress and well-being: a systematic review and meta-analysis (Goyal et al.)","publisher":"JAMA Internal Medicine, via PubMed (NIH)","year":2014,"url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24395196/"},{"title":"A wandering mind is an unhappy mind (Killingsworth & Gilbert)","publisher":"Science","year":2010,"url":"https://dash.harvard.edu/handle/1/33431727"}]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, array['mental wellness','guided exercise']::text[], false, '', 'If stress, anxiety, or low mood is persistent or hard to manage, please talk with a licensed mental health professional. Mindfulness practices are not a substitute for care. If you are in crisis in the U.S., call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline). In an emergency, call 911.', '', 4, true, true, now()) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.pages (slug, title, summary, body, needs_professional_review) values ('manifesto', 'The Choice That Changes the View', 'Our founding message: you may not choose every circumstance, but you can learn to choose how you meet it.', 'You may not choose every circumstance. You can learn to choose how you meet it.

Life doesn''t always unfold the way we hoped. We experience loss, disappointment, uncertainty, rejection, and moments when the future feels difficult to imagine.

Choosing happiness does not mean denying these experiences, forcing a smile, or blaming ourselves for feeling sad.

It means giving ourselves permission to consider another perspective.

What else might be true? What remains within my control? What can I learn? Who can help me? What is one small step I can take today?

A difficult moment can be real without defining your entire life. A setback can hurt without determining your entire future. And asking for help can be an act of strength rather than a sign of failure.

You don''t have to feel happy every day to build a life with more meaning, connection, and hope.

The journey begins when you recognize that another perspective may be possible.', false) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.pages (slug, title, summary, body, needs_professional_review) values ('about', 'About & Mission', 'Who we are, what #ChooseHappiness is for, and just as importantly what it is not.', '## Our mission

#ChooseHappiness exists to help improve society''s mental well-being and personal life perspectives through accessible education, thoughtful reflection, healthy coping practices, community, and encouragement.

## Our philosophy

> Begin the journey to choose happiness when the paths are before you.

> Happiness isn''t about pretending life is perfect. It''s about discovering healthier ways to meet life as it is.

## What we are

- A mental wellness **education** website with plain-language articles, guided reflections, and daily prompts.
- A **peer-support community** where people can share experiences and encourage one another.
- A place to practice small, everyday skills such as noticing thoughts, pausing, reframing, and reaching out.

## What we are not

- **Not therapy or medical care.** Nothing here is a diagnosis, treatment, or a substitute for a licensed professional.
- **Not an emergency or crisis service.** We do not monitor the site around the clock and cannot send help to you. If you are in danger or thinking about harming yourself, please see [Get Help Now](/get-help).
- **Not staffed by counselors.** Community moderators are not crisis counselors or clinicians.
- **Not a promise of outcomes.** Different things help different people, and some difficulties call for professional support.

## Who started this

#ChooseHappiness was founded by Stephen A. Parks Jr. and Gina P. McCoy, an engaged couple.

## How we approach content

- Our content is **general education**, not personal advice.
- We **cite our sources** where we make research claims, and we try to say plainly how strong the evidence is. See [Research & Evidence](/research).
- Content that touches clinically sensitive topics is intended to be **reviewed by qualified professionals** before it is presented as clinical guidance. Until that review has happened, it is clearly marked as **not yet professionally reviewed**.
- We separate established findings, emerging evidence, opinion, and brand philosophy so you can tell which is which.', false) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.pages (slug, title, summary, body, needs_professional_review) values ('get-help', 'Get Help Now', 'If you are in crisis or worried about your safety, please use these services. This website is not one of them.', '## If you are in immediate danger

**Call 911** (in the U.S.) or your local emergency number. Do not wait for a reply on this website.

## If you are thinking about suicide or are in emotional distress (U.S.)

**988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline** (free, confidential, 24/7/365)

- **Call** 988
- **Text** 988
- **Chat** online at [chat.988lifeline.org](https://chat.988lifeline.org/)
- **Veterans and service members:** dial 988, then press 1, or text 838255 (the Veterans Crisis Line). Chat is also available at [veteranscrisisline.net](https://www.veteranscrisisline.net/).
- **Spanish:** 988 text and chat are available in Spanish at [988lifeline.org/es](https://988lifeline.org/es/inicio/).
- **Deaf and hard of hearing:** ASL callers can call 988 using a videophone (VP) number. TTY users can use their preferred relay service or dial 711, then 988. Details: [988lifeline.org/deaf-hard-of-hearing-hearing-loss](https://988lifeline.org/deaf-hard-of-hearing-hearing-loss/).

## Other support (U.S.)

- **Crisis Text Line:** text **HOME** to **741741** to reach a trained volunteer Crisis Counselor, 24/7, free and confidential. Text **HOLA** to 741741 for Spanish. ([crisistextline.org](https://www.crisistextline.org/))
- **SAMHSA National Helpline:** **1-800-662-HELP (4357)**, free and confidential, 24/7, English and Spanish. It is a treatment referral and information service for mental health and substance use. ([samhsa.gov](https://www.samhsa.gov/find-help/national-helpline))
- **Find treatment near you:** [findtreatment.gov](https://findtreatment.gov/) helps you locate state-licensed mental health and substance use treatment in the U.S. and its territories.

## Outside the United States

Call your local emergency number. You can also look up a helpline for your country at [findahelpline.com](https://findahelpline.com/), a free directory run by ThroughLine. Please note that the directory is not an emergency service itself.

## What this website can and cannot do

- This site is **not monitored 24 hours a day**.
- Our moderators are **volunteers or community staff, not crisis counselors**, and not clinicians.
- We **cannot send help to your location** and we do not know where you are.
- We **do not use automated tools to predict risk** or detect who might be in crisis. If you post something worrying, no one is guaranteed to see it in time.
- Posts and messages here are **not a substitute** for contacting a crisis service or emergency responders.

## If you are worried about someone else

- If they are in immediate danger, call 911 or your local emergency number.
- If you are unsure, you can call or text 988 yourself and ask for guidance on how to support them.
- Ask directly and kindly whether they are thinking about suicide. Asking does not plant the idea. Listen without judging, and stay with them or help them connect to someone who can.
- Encourage them to contact 988, a doctor, or a mental health professional, and offer to help make the call.
- Look after yourself too. Supporting someone is hard, and you are allowed to ask for help.

*Phone numbers and services can change. We last checked these details against the official websites linked above. If a number does not work, please use the official site.*', true) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.pages (slug, title, summary, body, needs_professional_review) values ('research', 'Research & Evidence', 'Plain-language summaries of what research suggests about five everyday practices, how strong the evidence is, and where it falls short.', 'This page summarizes research in plain language. It is education, not medical advice. Where we say a study found something, we only report numbers we read in the source itself. Effects in this field are often small to moderate, they vary between people, and these practices are not a replacement for professional care.

## How we label content

- **Established findings:** results seen repeatedly across many studies, ideally with systematic reviews or meta-analyses. Even then we say what the finding does and does not show.
- **Emerging evidence:** early or limited results, small studies, or findings that have not yet been consistently replicated.
- **Opinion:** the views of our writers or community members. Useful, but not research.
- **Brand philosophy:** our guiding beliefs, such as our manifesto. These are values, not scientific claims.

**Strength of evidence labels** used below: *Established*, *Promising*, *Emerging*, *Mixed*. These are our own plain-language judgments based on the sources listed, and they are not a formal grading. They have not yet been reviewed by a qualified professional.

---

## Social connection

**What research suggests.** A large meta-analysis of 148 studies with 308,849 participants found that people with stronger social relationships had a 50% greater likelihood of survival over the follow-up periods (odds ratio 1.50). The U.S. Surgeon General''s advisory also describes links between social connection and health, including depression.

**Strength of evidence:** Established (as an association).

**Limitations.** Most of the evidence is observational, and the authors state that "causality is not easily established." Results varied a lot between studies, most measured relationships with simple measures that did not capture quality, and most data came from North America and Western Europe. Being around people does not help if the relationships are harmful.

**Sources**
- Holt-Lunstad J, Smith TB, Layton JB. Social Relationships and Mortality Risk: A Meta-analytic Review. PLOS Medicine, 2010. [Link](https://journals.plos.org/plosmedicine/article/info:doi/10.1371/journal.pmed.1000316)
- Office of the U.S. Surgeon General. Social Connection (advisory and fact cards). [Link](https://www.hhs.gov/surgeongeneral/reports-and-publications/connection/index.html)

---

## Mindfulness

**What research suggests.** A review of 47 trials with 3,515 participants found moderate evidence that mindfulness meditation programs improve anxiety and depression, and low evidence for stress and distress. NCCIH notes mindfulness is generally considered low risk, though about 8% of participants in studies in one review reported a negative experience.

**Strength of evidence:** Mixed.

**Limitations.** NCCIH states that "much of the research on these topics has been preliminary or not scientifically rigorous," and results "may have been interpreted too optimistically." Studies that followed people beyond about two months in one analysis found no lasting effects. Mindfulness can be uncomfortable for some people, especially those with trauma, and should not replace conventional care.

**Sources**
- Goyal M et al. Meditation programs for psychological stress and well-being: a systematic review and meta-analysis. JAMA Internal Medicine, 2014. [Link](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24395196/)
- NCCIH. Meditation and Mindfulness: Effectiveness and Safety. [Link](https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/meditation-and-mindfulness-what-you-need-to-know)

---

## Cognitive reappraisal (reframing)

**What research suggests.** A meta-analysis of 306 experimental comparisons found that strategies involving cognitive change, such as reappraising a situation or taking another person''s perspective, had small to medium effects on emotional outcomes (overall d+ = 0.36). Attentional strategies overall showed no average effect, and suppressing thoughts of an upsetting event was slightly negative.

**Strength of evidence:** Promising.

**Limitations.** Most of the included studies were experiments that manipulated emotions for a short time, often with students or volunteers. They do not show how reframing works over months in real life, or for people with serious distress. How you reframe matters, and reframing is not about denying that something is hard.

**Sources**
- Webb TL, Miles E, Sheeran P. Dealing with feeling: a meta-analysis of the effectiveness of strategies derived from the process model of emotion regulation. Psychological Bulletin, 2012. [Link](https://sussex.figshare.com/articles/journal_contribution/Dealing_with_feeling_a_meta-analysis_of_the_effectiveness_of_strategies_derived_from_the_process_model_of_emotion_regulation/23396432)

---

## Gratitude practices

**What research suggests.** A meta-analysis of 27 studies with 3,675 participants found gratitude interventions had a small effect on symptoms of depression and anxiety (g = -0.29) right after the intervention.

**Strength of evidence:** Promising (small effects).

**Limitations.** The authors note that earlier reviews raised the possibility that benefits partly reflect placebo effects. The effect is small, and gratitude exercises are not a treatment for depression or anxiety. For some people, being told to "be grateful" can feel dismissive of real pain, so we encourage appreciating things without dismissing what is difficult.

**Sources**
- Cregg DR, Cheavens JS. Gratitude Interventions: Effective Self-help? A Meta-analysis of the Impact on Symptoms of Depression and Anxiety. Journal of Happiness Studies, 2021. [Link](https://ideas.repec.org/a/spr/jhappi/v22y2021i1d10.1007_s10902-020-00236-6.html)

---

## Self-compassion

**What research suggests.** A meta-analysis of 27 randomized controlled trials found that self-compassion interventions were linked to moderate improvements in outcomes such as stress (g = 0.67), depression (g = 0.66), anxiety (g = 0.57), and self-criticism (g = 0.56).

**Strength of evidence:** Promising.

**Limitations.** The authors say publication bias may be present. The interventions were quite different from one another, and results varied by population. Group-delivered programs showed stronger effects than individual ones. Our summary is based on the published abstract, since the full text was not available to us.

**Sources**
- Ferrari M et al. Self-compassion interventions and psychosocial outcomes: a meta-analysis of RCTs. Mindfulness, 2019. [Link](https://acuresearchbank.acu.edu.au/item/87545/self-compassion-interventions-and-psychosocial-outcomes-a-meta-analysis-of-rcts)

---

*Research changes. If you spot an error or a newer study, please tell us so we can update this page.*', true) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.pages (slug, title, summary, body, needs_professional_review) values ('privacy', 'Privacy Policy (DRAFT)', 'DRAFT privacy policy describing how #ChooseHappiness handles your information. Pending qualified legal review.', '> **DRAFT: NOT YET LEGALLY REVIEWED.** This document is a working draft and must be reviewed by a qualified attorney before launch. Bracketed items are placeholders the owners must fill in.

*Last updated: [date]*

## 1. Who we are

#ChooseHappiness ("we," "us") is a mental wellness education and peer-support website operated by its founders, Stephen A. Parks Jr. and Gina P. McCoy. Contact: [owner email address].

This is not a health care provider. We are **not a HIPAA covered entity**, and we do not claim HIPAA compliance or any security certification.

## 2. Information we collect

- **Account information:** email address, display name, and password (stored in hashed form by our authentication provider).
- **Content you create:** private journal entries, community posts and replies, chat messages, and any files you upload.
- **Reports and moderation records:** reports you submit and actions taken on content or accounts.
- **Basic technical data** needed to run the service, such as session information.

## 3. Where your data is stored

We use **Supabase** to provide authentication, a Postgres database, file storage, and realtime features. Your data is hosted with Supabase and its infrastructure providers under their terms.

## 4. Private journal entries

- Journal entries are protected by **row-level security** so that only **you** can read your own entries through the app.
- **Administrators and moderators cannot read your journal entries through the app.**
- However, people with **database-level or infrastructure access** could technically read stored data. This includes our hosting provider and the owners'' project administrators who hold service credentials. We do not intend to access journal entries, and we [describe internal access policy here].
- We may **disclose information if legally required**, such as in response to valid legal process.
- Journal entries are not end-to-end encrypted.

## 5. Community content, reports, and messages

Community posts are visible to other members according to the settings of each space. When you **report** a post or message, it becomes visible to moderators so they can review it. Chat messages are kept for [X days], then deleted.

## 6. What we do not do

- We do **not** show advertising.
- We do **not** sell your personal data.
- We do **not** use third-party tracking or analytics by default. If we add analytics in the future, we will update this policy, and we will not send sensitive content (such as journal text or messages) to analytics tools.
- We do **not** use automated tools to predict or monitor your risk.

## 7. Email

We send **transactional emails only**, such as sign-in links, password resets, and account notices. We do not send marketing email unless you opt in.

## 8. Your choices

In **Account settings** you can **export your data** and **delete your account**.

## 9. Retention

When you delete content or your account, the content is removed from our active systems. [Describe any backup retention period.] Chat message retention is set by the owners at [X days]. Moderation records may be kept for [period] to enforce our rules.

## 10. Security

We use reasonable technical measures, including access controls, but no system is perfectly secure and we cannot guarantee absolute security.

## 11. Children and teens

Member accounts are for **adults 18 and older**. We do not knowingly collect information from anyone under 18; if we learn that we have, we will delete it. Visitors under 18 can still read the public educational pages without an account. *[Legal review needed: COPPA, state minor-privacy laws, and whether to allow teen accounts in the future.]*

## 12. Emergencies

We do not monitor accounts for emergencies. If you are in crisis, see [Get Help Now](/get-help).

## 13. Changes to this policy

We will post changes here and update the date above. [Describe notice for material changes.]

## 14. Contact

[Owner email address]', true) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.pages (slug, title, summary, body, needs_professional_review) values ('terms', 'Terms of Use (DRAFT)', 'DRAFT terms of use for #ChooseHappiness. Pending qualified legal review.', '> **DRAFT: NOT YET LEGALLY REVIEWED.** This document must be reviewed by a qualified attorney before launch. Bracketed items are placeholders.

*Last updated: [date]*

## 1. Acceptance

By creating an account or using #ChooseHappiness, you agree to these Terms and our Privacy Policy and Community Guidelines. If you do not agree, please do not use the site. You must be at least 18 years old to create an account. *[Legal review: age requirement and any future teen access with parental consent.]*

## 2. Wellness education only

#ChooseHappiness provides general wellness **education** and peer-support community features. It is **not medical advice, diagnosis, therapy, or treatment**, and it does not create a professional-client relationship. Always seek the advice of a qualified professional about your health. Do not delay or disregard professional advice because of something you read here.

## 3. Not an emergency service

We are **not an emergency or crisis service**. We do not monitor the site around the clock, and moderators are not crisis counselors. If you are in danger or considering harming yourself, call 911 or your local emergency number, or call or text 988 in the U.S. See [Get Help Now](/get-help).

## 4. Your account

You are responsible for your account and for keeping your login secure. Provide accurate information and do not share your account.

## 5. Community rules

You agree to follow the [Community Guidelines](/guidelines). Peer support is not professional care. Other members'' views are their own and we do not endorse them.

## 6. Your content and license

You keep ownership of what you post. You give us a limited, non-exclusive license to host, display, and transmit your content solely to operate the service and show it to the audience you choose. Private journal entries are visible only to you through the app. See the Privacy Policy for important details. The license ends when you delete the content, except for copies that must remain for a limited time in backups or as needed for legal or safety reasons.

## 7. Moderation, suspension, and appeals

We may remove content and warn, suspend, or ban accounts that break these Terms or the Guidelines or that put people at risk. You may appeal by contacting [owner email address]. We will review appeals in good faith, though decisions are at our discretion.

## 8. Termination

You may delete your account at any time in Account settings. We may suspend or end access to the service, with or without notice, for violations or to protect the community.

## 9. Disclaimers

The service is provided "as is" and "as available." To the fullest extent permitted by law, we disclaim warranties of any kind, including accuracy, availability, and fitness for a particular purpose. We do not guarantee any outcome from using the site. *[Legal review.]*

## 10. Limitation of liability

To the fullest extent permitted by law, we are not liable for indirect, incidental, or consequential damages arising from your use of the service. *[Legal review: scope and enforceability.]*

## 11. Governing law

These Terms are governed by the laws of the State of [State], without regard to conflict-of-law rules. *[Legal review: venue, dispute resolution.]*

## 12. Changes

We may update these Terms. Continued use after changes means you accept them.

## 13. Contact

[Owner email address]', true) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.pages (slug, title, summary, body, needs_professional_review) values ('guidelines', 'Community Guidelines', 'How we treat each other here, what moderators do and do not do, and what happens when rules are broken.', '#ChooseHappiness is a space for encouragement and shared experience. These guidelines help it stay kind and safe.

## How we show up

- **Be kind.** Assume good intent, listen, and disagree gently.
- **Share experiences, not diagnoses or medical advice.** Say "this helped me" instead of "you have..." or "you should stop taking...". Do not tell others to start or stop medication or treatment.
- **Do not promote self-harm, suicide, or methods.** No details about methods, and no encouragement of harming oneself or others. If you are struggling, please see [Get Help Now](/get-help).
- **Use content warnings** for heavy topics such as loss, violence, abuse, or self-harm, so people can choose whether to read.
- **Protect privacy.** Do not share other people''s personal information, private messages, or identifying details. Be careful with your own.
- **No harassment or hate.** No bullying, threats, slurs, or attacks based on race, ethnicity, religion, gender, sexual orientation, disability, or other identity.
- **No spam or selling.** No advertising, solicitation, or promoting products and services.

## Peer support is not professional care

Members are not therapists, and you should not treat what you read as professional advice. Support from peers can be valuable alongside professional help, not instead of it.

## How reporting works

Use the **Report** button on any post or message. A report makes that content visible to moderators, who review it against these guidelines. If you or someone else is in immediate danger, do not rely on a report. Call 911 or your local emergency number.

## What moderators do and do not do

**Moderators do:** review reported content, remove content that breaks the guidelines, send warnings, and suspend or ban accounts when needed.

**Moderators do not:** provide counseling or crisis support, diagnose, give medical advice, read private journal entries through the app, or watch the site around the clock. Unless the owners say otherwise on this page, moderation is **not 24/7**, and review may take time.

## Consequences

Depending on the situation, we may take these steps:

1. Content removal
2. Warning
3. Suspension
4. Ban

Serious violations, such as threats or encouraging self-harm, may lead to immediate suspension or ban.

## Appeals

If you think we made a mistake, you can appeal by contacting [owner email address] and explaining what happened. We will review it in good faith.', false) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.pages (slug, title, summary, body, needs_professional_review) values ('evidence-summary', 'What research suggests', 'Short evidence notes shown under the manifesto. Body is JSON.', '[
  {
    "topic": "Social connection",
    "finding": "A meta-analysis of 148 studies (308,849 participants) found people with stronger social relationships had a 50% greater likelihood of survival (OR 1.50).",
    "strength": "Established",
    "limitation": "The evidence is observational, so the authors note causality is not easily established.",
    "source_title": "Holt-Lunstad, Smith & Layton (2010), PLOS Medicine",
    "source_url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosmedicine/article/info:doi/10.1371/journal.pmed.1000316"
  },
  {
    "topic": "Mindfulness",
    "finding": "A review of 47 trials found moderate evidence that mindfulness meditation programs improve anxiety and depression, and low evidence for stress.",
    "strength": "Mixed",
    "limitation": "NCCIH notes much of the research has been preliminary or not scientifically rigorous.",
    "source_title": "Goyal et al. (2014), JAMA Internal Medicine",
    "source_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24395196/"
  },
  {
    "topic": "Cognitive reappraisal",
    "finding": "A meta-analysis of 306 experimental comparisons found cognitive change strategies had a small-to-medium effect on emotional outcomes (d+ = 0.36).",
    "strength": "Promising",
    "limitation": "Most studies were short-term lab experiments, so real-world, long-term effects are less clear.",
    "source_title": "Webb, Miles & Sheeran (2012), Psychological Bulletin",
    "source_url": "https://sussex.figshare.com/articles/journal_contribution/Dealing_with_feeling_a_meta-analysis_of_the_effectiveness_of_strategies_derived_from_the_process_model_of_emotion_regulation/23396432"
  },
  {
    "topic": "Gratitude",
    "finding": "A meta-analysis of 27 studies found gratitude interventions had a small effect on depression and anxiety symptoms right after the intervention (g = -0.29).",
    "strength": "Promising",
    "limitation": "Effects are small, and earlier reviews raised the possibility of placebo effects.",
    "source_title": "Cregg & Cheavens (2021), Journal of Happiness Studies",
    "source_url": "https://ideas.repec.org/a/spr/jhappi/v22y2021i1d10.1007_s10902-020-00236-6.html"
  },
  {
    "topic": "Self-compassion",
    "finding": "A meta-analysis of 27 randomized trials found moderate improvements in stress (g = 0.67), depression (g = 0.66), and anxiety (g = 0.57).",
    "strength": "Promising",
    "limitation": "The authors say publication bias may be present and the interventions varied widely.",
    "source_title": "Ferrari et al. (2019), Mindfulness",
    "source_url": "https://acuresearchbank.acu.edu.au/item/87545/self-compassion-interventions-and-psychosocial-outcomes-a-meta-analysis-of-rcts"
  }
]', true) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.reflection_prompts (text) select x from unnest(array['What deserves a little more of my attention today?','What is one thing I can influence?','What assumption could I examine more carefully?','What would I say to a friend in my situation?','What is one thing I appreciate without dismissing what is difficult?','What small action would support my well-being today?','What is one kind thing I can say to myself right now?']::text[]) x where not exists (select 1 from public.reflection_prompts);
